WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.900
OK.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.400
Maybe we shall get started.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.820
This is the second of the
post-training lectures,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.680
so we're going to talk about the
exciting developments in RLVR

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:19.840
or reinforcement learning
from verifiable rewards.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.400
And I think this is in
some ways very timely.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.100
I didn't get to
update the slide,

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.360
but there was just a
announcement by OpenAI folks

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.800
that they had solved one
of the major problems.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.400
So that's a MyOpenMath
problem using

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.618
one of their thinking models.

00:00:37.618 --> 00:00:38.660
How does that thing work?

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.160
That's exactly this thinking
model stuff or RLVR stuff.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.760
And so thus far, where we're at
is we are at the left side here.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:52.580
After we finished last lecture,
we've got instruction tuning,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.480
we've got RLHF, we've got some
good stuff that's ChatGPT.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.970
And what remains is the modern
developments in thinking models.

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.790
The model ability
to do very long COT

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.150
and solve these hard,
verifiable problems

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.790
like mathematics and coding
in some cases as well.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.950
And remember that I ended last
lecture with this a little bit

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.710
of a downer note.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.343
The RLHF wasn't
really going to get us

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:21.510
where we wanted to go because
of this problem called

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.710
overoptimization.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.570
So we're going to go and collect
a bunch of preference data.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.390
Preference data is very useful.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.150
We will build some
reward model and then

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:31.990
we will RL against
that reward model.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.970
But this thing is fundamentally
very annotation bottleneck

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.710
because we have a
reward model and you

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.330
can't keep putting compute
into the same reward model,

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:43.030
eventually you're going to
overfit your reward model.

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.950
And no matter how good of a
job you do at regularizing,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.950
eventually you're going
to run into this problem

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.990
with overfitting.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.490
And so really, this
is very depressing.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.270
And it's not really
the potential

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.390
of reinforcement learning.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.390
If you're a big reinforcement
learning believer,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.220
you're going to look at
all things like AlphaGo

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.660
and so on where you say,
well, in these domains,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.260
RL really did an amazing job.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.820
What is the difference between
what we're doing in RLHF

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.540
and what has been
achieved in these more RL

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.380
native or RL natural domains?

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.600
And really the
difference, I would say,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.300
or one major difference
between something like AlphaGo

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.820
and something like RLHF is that
in AlphaGo, we are optimizing

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.500
exactly what we want.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:28.260
You get the win-loss
conditions of the game of Go.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.120
You don't have any sloppiness
to that definition.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.560
And so you can just put in
as much compute as you want.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.580
And as long as the objective
improves, you're doing well.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:40.000
In some sense, these
are search problems.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.860
Whereas the top one is much
more of a learning problem.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.060
Not quite a precise
distinction, but that's

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.580
one way of thinking about it.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.220
So now there might be other
domains like formal mathematics

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.220
or even natural
language mathematics,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.940
that have this flavor of being
more verifiable and therefore

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.230
much more amenable to
reinforcement learning.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.770
So that's going to be the
motivation for why we're

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:01.110
going to study some of these.

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.890
And the algorithms aren't
going to be that different

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.490
fundamentally, but
where we will end up

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.890
will actually be
surprisingly different.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.750
And so the lecture is going
to be in two parts today.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.610
This mirrors a lot of
how we've structured

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.690
the course in other parts.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.197
So in the first
part of the lecture,

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:20.030
I'm going to talk about
the core algorithms.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.370
This is like basic
knowledge for you all.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:25.020
You will use some of this
for your assignments.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.770
And so I'm going to
talk about things what

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:29.130
is GRPO, how does PPO work.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.570
And then after
I've done that, I'm

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.090
going to dig through a bunch
of open source releases

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.850
and show you how,
what I've taught you

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:40.690
in the first part of reflects
in the technical reports of many

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:46.000
of these major open
source model releases.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.410
So we're going to dive in to
the methods and basic section.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.190
And the first thing that I'm
going to start with is PPO.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.560
We can't really discuss
RL for language models

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.642
without discussing PPO.

00:03:58.642 --> 00:04:00.100
And even though
we've done it once,

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.120
PPO is confusing enough
that I think you will

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.960
benefit from doing it twice.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.220
The most important
thing to identity,

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.880
let's say to remember in
RL for language modeling is

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:11.860
policy gradients.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.180
And in particular this
reinforce gradient trick.

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.560
Well, we are always going to
be doing is gradient descent

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.220
on our rewards.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.800
And we are going to do so by
taking essentially weighted SFT

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:26.390
updates.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.140
The weights might be
positive or negative.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.640
We will define what R is.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.880
But this equation is important
because it will reappear

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:34.252
throughout this lecture.

00:04:34.252 --> 00:04:35.960
This is in some sense
the core from which

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:38.120
everything else is derived.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.360
Now, building upon
that, remember

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:41.880
that the intuition
from last lecture

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.380
was we want to take more
than one step at a time.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.320
A policy gradient requires
us to sample from our policy

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.440
every time we want to
take a gradient step.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.020
Can we reuse our rollouts?

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.630
Well, you can do TRPO.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.850
You can do PPO and
so on and so forth.

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.690
I'll talk about PPO in a minute.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.490
And I've already
talked about TRPO.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.070
I won't really get
too much into this.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.190
If you've taken RL
course, PPO is, of course,

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:07.990
well understood to you, like
you understand this algorithm,

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:11.110
but I don't assume that all
of you have taken RL class

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.870
and/or understand RL.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.430
And so PPO, in case
you're not familiar,

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:20.450
is a real workhorse of RL.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:24.390
It's been used in a variety
of very difficult RL settings.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:28.230
OpenAI has done quite a few
of these in their early days

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.350
of being very RL pilled.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.710
They had, this was
I think, OpenAI

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.015
gym where they were talking
about the PPO algorithm

00:05:37.015 --> 00:05:38.390
and they're like,
hey, we can get

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:42.050
these people to walk around
using reinforcement learning.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.990
In a much more dramatic
demonstration of this

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.430
was their OpenAI bot, which
they trained using PPO.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:50.390
And so this can
excel with deep RL

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:53.720
in high-dimensional
action and state spaces,

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:58.740
where it's much more complicated
to do RL than it is usually.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:05:59.520
Oh, yeah.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.280
And there's the OpenAI
man running around.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.380
All right.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.300
So at a conceptual level,
PPO is actually very simple.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:13.100
Remember that I told you, an
important part of all of this

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.400
is getting rid of PPO.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.640
That's been a motivating
factor for a lot of people.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:18.900
But if you look at
the pseudocode of PPO,

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:23.555
this is from the OpenAI's like
spinning up on RL documentation.

00:06:23.555 --> 00:06:25.680
You look at this and you
say, this is not that bad.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:26.880
This is actually pretty easy.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.255
I could implement
this in one go.

00:06:28.255 --> 00:06:30.120
You sample some trajectories.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.860
I compute something called
an advantage using any method

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.480
of advantage estimation.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.780
I clip the advantage in this
slightly strange but OK way.

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.200
And then I update the policy
under this clipped advantage.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:42.867
And that's fine.

00:06:42.867 --> 00:06:45.408
And then I can fit something
called a value function as well.

00:06:45.408 --> 00:06:46.040
OK, fine.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.010
This is all relatively
easy looking in practice.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:54.110
And so I think because of this,
people are like, oh, it's OK.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:55.550
PPO is totally fine.

00:06:55.550 --> 00:06:57.550
But then you see blog
posts that look like this,

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.690
and this should strike
fear into your heart.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:03.250
Because if you see a blog post
that says the 37 implementation

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.930
details of PPO,
you know that this

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.730
is an algorithm that is very
sensitive to your implementation

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.730
decisions.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.735
And there's all sorts of
things that you can do

00:07:13.735 --> 00:07:16.110
and all sorts of different
libraries and implementations,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.210
and they give totally
different numbers

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.730
based on which ones you use.

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.090
And it turns out
that many people

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.270
have implemented this wrong.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.020
And so there's paper
saying, like actually

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.222
the baselines that
some people use in PPO

00:07:28.222 --> 00:07:29.430
aren't even baselines at all.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.810
They fundamentally change
the optimization problem.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:36.130
And so basically
because of this, I

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.610
think I would not be able to get
through the rest of the lecture

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.410
without telling you,
well, we actually

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.730
maybe need to start looking at
some implementations of PPO.

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:47.940
And the implementations
of PPO, let me tell you,

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.120
for language models are
not particularly pleasant.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.100
This is, I mean, a very
good representative slide.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.080
If anything, this is a
very good description

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:57.840
of what PPO looks like.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.163
You've got advantage estimation.

00:08:00.163 --> 00:08:02.580
This big block in the middle,
that's this advantage thing.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.400
You've got an experienced
buffer because you're going

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.828
to keep some of the old stuff.

00:08:05.828 --> 00:08:07.620
It's going to be training
this value model.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.640
And the value models are being
used in the advantage estimation

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:11.300
calculation.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:14.480
Notice how the green
box appears twice.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.360
And importantly, some parts
of my objective, the KL term,

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.500
actually operates
token by token.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.420
So it's not actually
just a bandit problem.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.940
It's like a whole
multi-step RL problem.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.960
And this is all very
difficult and complicated.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:33.280
And so this has led to a variety
of really tricky implementation

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.780
things that you have to do.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.799
And if you look at a
variety of implementations,

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:40.320
you find often that
there are things

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.840
that people do for PPO that are
a little bit strange and tricky

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:45.710
at times.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:48.710
Let me see if I can find.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:50.430
So one of my
students implemented

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:53.550
a PPO for a project a
while back doing RLHF.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:56.070
So PPO, if you remember,
was used in RLHF.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.225
And so we had this nice robust
reference implementation of PPO

00:09:00.225 --> 00:09:01.850
and it took a long
time to get working.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:03.910
So I looked into the details,
like what did we actually

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:05.290
do to get this thing to work.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:06.248
The outer loop is fine.

00:09:06.248 --> 00:09:08.790
The outer loop is something like
you go through your rollouts

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:10.570
and you're going to
take some PPO steps,

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.430
you're going to compute some
loss and clip some gradients

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:13.290
and take some steps.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.590
Totally reasonable outer loop.

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:18.830
But then you start
of looking into it.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:22.030
For now, the actual
inner compute loss part

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:24.890
follows almost exactly
the PPO update.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:26.770
So this also looks mostly good.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:29.510
We're computing things
like the advantages

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:32.170
and we're computing
the clipping ratios.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.410
And then we're updating
the model based on that.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.590
So this is all good.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:40.390
But then you get to some
of the messier parts.

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:42.590
Actually, I'll
skip the rollouts,

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.410
where you have things
like well, maybe we

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:49.050
need KL penalties to
keep the original model

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.130
close to the reference,
but actually this

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.950
only works if you clip the
KL off at 0, which of course,

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.730
if you know anything about KL
divergences, that totally ruins

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:58.090
the point of a KL divergence.

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.850
You have both positive and
negative values being summed.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.330
If you remove this, it
blows up immediately.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.863
All things like this can happen.

00:10:04.863 --> 00:10:07.530
This is not to say that this is
the way you should implement PPO

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:08.670
if you were doing it.

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:10.490
I think the reason why
I'm bringing this up

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.530
is to say implementations
for RL algorithms can

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:16.210
be somewhat tricky and somewhat
sensitive because of all

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:18.010
the moving pieces
in PPO, and because

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:20.610
of the high variance in
the gradient estimates.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:25.290
These are all very, very tricky
things that are happening.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.710
And, another thing
that you often see,

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:29.550
this one is not just
RL implementation.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.053
It's very common.

00:10:31.053 --> 00:10:32.970
In the original PPO
paper, you have this thing

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.930
called a generalized advantage
estimator that's basically

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:39.770
looking at this gamma discounted
reward, where you have

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.690
this value function
that's estimating

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:44.800
the reward at of every
token generation step.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.040
But it turns out that
people often just

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.240
use gamma equals lambda
equals 1, which is just

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.300
a degenerate setting that turns
this back into a bandit problem.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.480
So you've thrown away
a lot of the structure

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.140
that you get from PPO.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.740
And so if you run this algorithm
after a bunch of engineering,

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:03.300
you will get what you expect.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:06.780
And when you run your RL
algorithms for the assignment,

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:09.860
you should expect to see similar
things like the rewards go up.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.880
The reward model rewards
go up if you're doing RLHF,

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:15.980
and then the negative
KL regularizers go down.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:19.080
This is a reasonable
thing to expect to see.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.760
But anyway, the
point of all of this

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:24.960
is to say implementation of
PPO can be quite painful.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.660
Depending on the environment, it
can require hacks to stabilize.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:31.083
All of this is painful.

00:11:31.083 --> 00:11:32.500
Not to say that
it's not possible.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:36.640
Lots of people have trained
very good models on PPO.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.840
Many of the labs have
very turnkey solutions

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.710
for getting PPOs
to work at scale.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:44.790
So this is not impossible.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.790
But for people like researchers
who are implementing this

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:51.870
from scratch, PPO can just be
really finicky and complicated.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:55.310
Another reason why PPO is
disfavored in many cases

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:58.710
is that it requires a value
model to estimate the value

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.590
at each token as you go.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:02.170
And how big is the value model?

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:04.410
Well, it's as big as
the original model,

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.710
so this consumes some
memory that you would rather

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:11.990
be using for other stuff like
models or inference servers.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.590
So now you might say,
well, in last lecture,

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.650
you told us about
this DPO thing,

00:12:16.650 --> 00:12:18.630
and DPO seems pretty good.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.410
Why don't we just use
DPO for everything?

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.590
Well, the problem is DPO
is a very specific solution

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.130
to a very specific problem.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:28.030
DPO is good for pairwise
feedback in the form

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:29.610
of Bradley-Terry comparisons.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:31.230
That is very specific.

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:33.590
And if I want to
solve math problems,

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:36.470
my math problems don't come in
the form of inherently pairwise

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.210
comparisons.

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:39.140
There are other
DPO variants that

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:41.640
are designed to break
that pairwise structure,

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.900
but really you're just using
the wrong hammer for the job.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.460
You do not want to
use DPO necessarily

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:49.660
for what you would
normally use PPO for.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.460
PPO is the more general hammer
that you can hit everything

00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.660
with.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.080
DPO is generally offline.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.100
Although I think
this distinction

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:59.020
is very overstated
because it can

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.520
be made online by just
iterating DPO repeatedly.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:04.980
So I don't think this is
necessarily a big difference.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:09.980
So that's the context
for all of this.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:13.080
And then so that
similarly to DPO,

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:16.060
there's just an enormous desire
by the research community

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.500
to not have to use PPO.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:22.300
Hopefully, the fact that DPO
and GRPO have gotten adoption

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:26.220
tells you how painful it was to
get this to work in many cases.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:29.240
And so GRPO much like
DPO, is the alternative,

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:33.320
it is the simpler way to
do RL for verifiable task.

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:35.820
It has taken over
for the most part

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:39.470
RLVR in the open
source, open knowledge,

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.450
let's call it community.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.110
And it is very, very
similar to PPO in spirit.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:47.270
It just strips out a couple
of the most complicated parts,

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:51.130
and by doing so, allows us to
have a much simpler algorithm.

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:55.410
So GRPO is by DeepSeek paper.

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:57.970
I think it was there
like DeepSeek math paper,

00:13:57.970 --> 00:13:59.690
where you start with PPO.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:02.270
It conceptually it says
PPO is a good idea,

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:04.110
but we want to change
just a few things.

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:05.110
And what does it change?

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.290
It changes arguably the most
complicated and annoying part

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:10.170
of PPO, which is
the value function.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:11.890
So the value
function is the thing

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:14.930
that you subtract as a
baseline to reduce variance

00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:17.035
of your gradient updates.

00:14:17.035 --> 00:14:18.910
The value function is
a whole neural network.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:20.110
It destabilizes training.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:21.930
We don't want the value network.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:23.167
So what do we do?

00:14:23.167 --> 00:14:24.750
Well, we get rid of
the value network,

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.230
but we still need an advantage.

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.133
I don't want to do reinforce--

00:14:28.133 --> 00:14:30.050
vanilla reinforce because
the variance of that

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:32.050
is going to be
really, really high.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:33.930
So instead, what
we're going to do

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:36.000
is I'm going to
compute you advantage

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.840
as a z-score within a
group, and I'll explain

00:14:38.840 --> 00:14:40.640
what that means in a moment.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.080
But basically, the
idea is the following.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:45.392
Normally, you look at
a reward that you get

00:14:45.392 --> 00:14:47.600
and if you had a value
function, you would compare it

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:49.197
to your predicted value.

00:14:49.197 --> 00:14:50.780
You would say, my
neural network says,

00:14:50.780 --> 00:14:52.460
I should have
gotten a score of 5.

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:53.500
I got a score of 6.

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:54.760
So this is a good rollout.

00:14:54.760 --> 00:14:56.360
That's usually what you do.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:14:58.720
Instead, what you
would do is you

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:01.420
would let's say your
rollout with a score of 5.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:03.120
You would now sample
10 other rollouts

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:04.960
and you would say, how
good was I compared

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.140
to my 10 other rollouts.

00:15:06.140 --> 00:15:08.240
If I'm doing better
than my mean,

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:10.440
then I have a high advantage.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:14.400
In some ways, a very natural
way of computing advantage

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.520
in contexts where you
can multiple sample

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:19.080
from the same prompt
or something like that.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.140
So GRPO is exactly this idea.

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:24.680
If you look at the initial
definition of GRPO that

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:27.420
appeared in the
DeepSeek paper, you see,

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:30.860
this is the objective
that's being optimized.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:34.590
They're going to do exactly
the PPO update, where they have

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.550
this min clipped
advantage, plus a KL term

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:40.590
to keep yourself close
to the reference.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:42.650
And then KL is computed
in a particular way.

00:15:42.650 --> 00:15:44.610
That detail is not
particularly important.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.630
And importantly,
this advantage is

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.910
computed as a z-score over
samples of a group of outputs,

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:51.710
o of 1 through o of g.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:54.437
So that's-- or within each
group, you have-- oh, sorry.

00:15:54.437 --> 00:15:56.270
This advantage of i,
this is the place where

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:57.353
you compute the advantage.

00:15:57.353 --> 00:16:00.150
What you do is you take the
reward over your rollouts

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.470
within each group,
you subtract the mean

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.710
and you divide by the
standard deviation.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.230
So you get a z-score
for each group.

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:11.045
So in the online case and this
is an important distinction

00:16:11.045 --> 00:16:12.670
because in many cases,
you may actually

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:15.790
end up running this online
rather than offline.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.030
In the online case,
the clipping just

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:22.190
disappears because the ratio
between pi theta old and pi

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:25.710
theta is 1, this clipping
operator never does anything

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.270
and you just get
min a of i, a of i.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:31.050
So this is just advantage
minus a KL penalty.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:32.940
So I'm going to do RL
on my advantage, which

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:33.920
is defined by this.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:35.580
So in the online
rollout case, it

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:41.840
is a very, very simple object
that makes sense as RL target.

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:43.860
So I can pause here
for a moment in case

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:46.280
any of the conceptual bits
of GRPO are not clear.

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.447
I mean, in some sense, this
is the foundation of all

00:16:48.447 --> 00:16:50.280
of the exciting math stuff.

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:54.132
Or you can replicate all of the
exciting math stuff using GRPO.

00:16:54.132 --> 00:16:56.340
So it's worth understanding
in a little bit of detail

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:59.640
before we move on to some
of the nitty-gritty details.

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:06.540
OK.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:07.200
Good.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:09.359
Simple enough that everyone
understands in one go.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.540
I will take it.

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:16.140
And GRPO is very, very simple
because of the lack of value

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.780
function, you can basically
write down all of GRPO

00:17:19.780 --> 00:17:22.839
in a very simple block of
code, where you just say,

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:25.260
I'm going to roll
out k times, I'm

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:29.760
going to compare my observed
rollout to those k references,

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:32.210
z-score it and then just
take a reinforced gradient

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:33.390
against those weights.

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:37.890
So you will write this
in your assignment.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.410
And it's going to involve
a very simple set of steps.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.570
Compute a reward for
each of your k rollouts.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:47.232
Normalize your rollouts,
compute the KL term.

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:48.690
And then just take
gradient updates

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:51.832
on the combination of your
KL term and these rollouts.

00:17:51.832 --> 00:17:54.290
You'll have to do a little bit
of thinking because in order

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:56.810
to do this using
autodiff, you will have

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:58.590
to do a stop grad somewhere.

00:17:58.590 --> 00:18:00.350
But it's actually
not that complicated.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:03.610
And you can see that you can
do a one-page implementation

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:07.090
of this basic idea.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:09.450
And this is a reference
implementation of GRPO

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.990
that I took from
some folks at McGill,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:17.250
which has a really nice little
toy version of this whole thing.

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.270
And the computation
of the group indices,

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:22.570
and the standard deviation
calculation and all of that

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:24.985
just fits in one
half of a slide.

00:18:24.985 --> 00:18:26.610
The only thing that
they do differently

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.000
than the literal
paper write up of GRPO

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:31.880
here is they add a little
tiny weeny negative 4

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:33.560
to the standard
deviation calculation

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:35.480
to prevent it from
blowing up when you only

00:18:35.480 --> 00:18:37.640
have a single sample, or
if your samples happen

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:41.040
to have the exact same rewards,
which does happen if you're

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.920
in a domain where you can get
exactly numerically 0 rewards,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.360
like you failed to
solve a math problem.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:49.800
But hopefully, this
emphasizes to you

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:54.880
how straightforward GRPO is,
and why almost all open source

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:56.780
work has been built
on this algorithm.

00:18:56.780 --> 00:18:59.200
It's easy to implement,
easy to understand.

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:01.540
And if you, as we'll
see in the results,

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.600
it provides fairly
compelling results

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.360
in terms of emulating a lot of
what the closed source labs have

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:12.240
done using potentially PPO.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.440
So how well does it work?

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:16.940
In the original
DeepSeek math paper,

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:19.300
which if you're
into math AI stuff,

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:20.800
I would encourage
you to read, it's

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:24.200
a nice and really
interesting set of results.

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:28.270
They show that GRPO, the
yellow and the blue lines,

00:19:28.270 --> 00:19:31.097
does much better than RFT,
like rejection fine tuning.

00:19:31.097 --> 00:19:32.930
You'll implement this
as a baseline as well.

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.990
That's just taking
the correct answers

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:36.950
that your model generates
and training on them

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:38.530
and throwing away
everything else.

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:42.830
And they seem to show
process supervision, which

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.730
is grading not just
the final answer,

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.290
but grading the intermediates
give you some gains.

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:48.690
I'll talk more about that later.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.630
That is an important
thing to discuss and cover

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:55.190
because that's one of
the big design decisions

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:56.810
for these kinds of RL problems.

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:59.550
But for now, you can
just internalize the fact

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:00.650
that GRPO works.

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:05.070
The blue and yellow lines
are above the others.

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.510
OK.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:10.050
So now let's think a little bit
more about the GRPO objective.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:11.930
What is happening
in this objective?

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:14.050
Are we actually taking
policy gradients?

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:17.470
There are important conceptual
questions that we should really

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.230
think through.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:23.210
And so if we think about the
difference between GRPO and PPO,

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:26.220
remember that PPO had this
value function thing which

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:27.580
feeds into the advantage.

00:20:27.580 --> 00:20:29.900
And in GRPO, we strip
that out and we replace

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:31.280
that with the z-score guy.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:33.940
This a of i, ratio
of r minus mean

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:36.180
divided by standard deviation.

00:20:36.180 --> 00:20:38.900
So is this a good advantage?

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:41.640
I think for those of you that
have been in an RL class,

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:43.840
you already know the
answer to this question.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:45.720
Is this a good
advantage function?

00:20:48.153 --> 00:20:49.320
What makes a good advantage?

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.300
What makes a valid
advantage function?

00:20:51.300 --> 00:20:55.980
Well, if you look at Sutton
and Barto, the big classic book

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:58.400
for reinforcement
learning they will say,

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:01.320
well, there's this algorithm
called reinforce with baseline.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.020
And the things
you're allowed to do

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:06.140
is that if you're doing
policy gradients, which

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.760
is this line over here,
this very first equation.

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:13.600
I want to take my gradient step
on the direction of my rewards.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.940
And the thing you can do is
you can take your rewards

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.700
and I can subtract
any-- what they

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.800
call state-dependent baseline,
in this case, our state.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.140
If we're in the bandit
world, it's just the prompt.

00:21:23.140 --> 00:21:25.730
So you can have a
prompt-dependent baseline

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:26.910
that you can subtract.

00:21:26.910 --> 00:21:30.770
And anything that does
just this is a valid form

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:31.760
of a policy gradient.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:34.010
So you're still going to
descend in the same direction

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:35.030
as long as you do this.

00:21:35.030 --> 00:21:36.930
And depending on
your choice of b,

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:40.490
you will either have
lower or higher variance

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:43.570
in doing this gradient
descent process.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:47.490
Now, you should probably
notice that what we're doing

00:21:47.490 --> 00:21:48.790
is not this.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:51.430
We are not just subtracting
a constant value,

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:55.490
we are also dividing by
the standard deviation.

00:21:55.490 --> 00:21:58.150
And so that is a problem.

00:21:58.150 --> 00:22:01.670
It turns out well, it's
in some ways a problem.

00:22:01.670 --> 00:22:02.910
In other ways, it's not.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:06.090
If you really want a
conceptually clear algorithm

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.450
that really does what's
written on the tin,

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.070
it actually descends the reward.

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:11.390
GRPO does not do that.

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:14.290
Because as I've
highlighted in red here,

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:17.250
GRPO divides by the
standard deviation, which

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:20.630
breaks this baseline contract.

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:23.540
We're not just dividing
or subtracting a baseline.

00:22:23.540 --> 00:22:27.000
We're also normalizing
by the standard deviation

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.740
of my rewards.

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:33.420
Of course, there's another
thing that GRPO does,

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:36.120
which I didn't mention earlier.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:39.920
As an implementation detail
that is tricky, which is GRPO

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.900
does this almost per token.

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:46.320
So they'll divide by the
total length of the sequence

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:48.300
as a normalization factor.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:51.940
But if you try to derive
GRPO from first principles,

00:22:51.940 --> 00:22:54.355
following the policy gradient
and baseline theorems,

00:22:54.355 --> 00:22:55.980
you'll end up with
something different.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.640
You won't have a
length normalizer

00:22:57.640 --> 00:23:00.780
and you won't have the standard
deviation normalization.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:05.560
And some folks soon after
GRPO came out noticed this,

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.800
and they wrote a paper
basically saying, well,

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:11.580
if you don't do these two
things that are different,

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:13.560
then you get very
different potentially very

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:14.700
positive behavior.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:15.660
I will talk about that.

00:23:15.660 --> 00:23:18.480
Also, when we get to the--

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.890
not OpenAI, sorry
DeepSeek R1 paper.

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:24.870
But the main thing
here is to say

00:23:24.870 --> 00:23:28.730
GRPO is not the first principles
derivation of this idea.

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:30.830
It is actually doing
something slightly different

00:23:30.830 --> 00:23:34.150
with both pros and cons.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.650
So what are these
two terms doing?

00:23:36.650 --> 00:23:40.590
We now that GRPO is not
actually directly descending

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:41.990
on the reward objective.

00:23:41.990 --> 00:23:45.450
We know that it has these
two correction factors.

00:23:45.450 --> 00:23:46.430
What do they do?

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:49.290
Well, the length normalization
is easier to see.

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:50.410
So what are we doing?

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:55.110
We are dividing longer
sequences with a bigger number.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:57.670
And so what this
will do is that it's

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:00.630
going to encourage
the model to generate

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:03.590
long outputs because-- or
sorry when you're wrong

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:06.002
it will encourage you to
generate wrong outputs.

00:24:06.002 --> 00:24:07.710
Because let's say--
I know that I'm going

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:08.793
to get a math proof wrong.

00:24:08.793 --> 00:24:11.750
I'm going to incur my negative
reward of let's say, negative 1.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.370
I'm just going to generate
an infinitely long string.

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:17.070
If I do that, I'll get
to divide by infinity,

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.900
and I'll get to totally get
rid of my negative penalty.

00:24:19.900 --> 00:24:21.240
That's an extreme case.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:24.000
But you understand that if
you divide by the output,

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.700
you encourage the model to blab
on once it realizes that it

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.160
cannot actually
solve the problem.

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:33.540
So this is a length problem
that you end up getting.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:37.140
If you fix that,
you'll find that often

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:41.400
what people have observed in
GRPO COT, like chain of thought,

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:43.800
thinking length growing
and growing and growing,

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.820
that actually turns out to
just cap off at a constant

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:49.040
rather than just continually
and forever grow.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:51.940
So this seems like a
potentially good thing.

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:53.905
And especially on
the incorrect cases,

00:24:53.905 --> 00:24:55.780
you really don't want
to be generating longer

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:59.020
and longer and longer outputs.

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:01.160
So the standard
deviation normalization,

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.280
this one's a little
bit more complicated.

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:05.420
But if you think about
it, what I'm doing

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:08.480
is I'm dividing by the
standard deviation.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.260
And so this can be
thought of as emphasizing

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:15.320
problems where the standard
deviation is small.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:17.330
And when is my
standard deviation

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.610
small for a binary
reward problem?

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:22.810
Well, that's when the problems
are too easy or too hard.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:26.850
If my problem is really easy
and I get 100% all the time,

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.890
I'm going to divide by
1 over or the number--

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.770
I'm going to divide
by basically 0

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:36.550
because there's 0 variation if
I always get a question correct.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:38.870
Similarly, if I always
get a question wrong,

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:41.170
I have 0 variation
in my rewards.

00:25:41.170 --> 00:25:43.210
I'm going to upweight
that significantly.

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:46.190
So the standard deviation term
is upweighting the two sides,

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:48.010
both easy and hard questions.

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:50.090
That seems like clearly
a thing that maybe we

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.530
don't want because we want our
models to potentially learn

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:56.930
on things that are within
its solvability range.

00:25:56.930 --> 00:26:01.850
So that's the algorithmic
components of RLVR.

00:26:01.850 --> 00:26:03.970
And I'm going to
go through several

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:06.410
of the different
recent model releases

00:26:06.410 --> 00:26:10.250
and talk through all the
algorithmic components

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:13.535
that I think are quite
important, including some

00:26:13.535 --> 00:26:16.160
of the stuff that I added this
year that's new, which is really

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.680
the agentic stuff that I
think has been catching on

00:26:18.680 --> 00:26:22.640
and is increasingly important
for using and deploying

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:23.182
these models.

00:26:23.182 --> 00:26:24.765
But before I do that,
I can pause here

00:26:24.765 --> 00:26:26.460
and if anyone has
algorithmic questions,

00:26:26.460 --> 00:26:28.220
I'll take them before I move on.

00:26:32.500 --> 00:26:33.000
OK.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.440
Good.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:35.840
All right.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:37.800
So we're going to
start with DeepSeek R1

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:41.340
because I think this is a
bit of a social phenomenon.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:44.660
I think everyone should know
a bunch about DeepSeek R1.

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:46.840
I think it's a lovely
paper kicked off

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:50.880
the wave of open
source RLVR models.

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:52.400
And what's remarkable
about it, I

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:55.120
think it was really
the first thing

00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:58.660
to have matched
OpenAI O1's behavior.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:01.560
So the key things
about OpenAI O1

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:03.780
was you have very long
chains of thought.

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:08.080
It's clearly RL, and you
have really good performance

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:09.740
on hard math problems.

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:14.570
And they also provided RL
recipe that anyone could do.

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:17.990
And I think from the perspective
of R&amp;D, this is very important.

00:27:17.990 --> 00:27:20.270
If your solution was like
you have this horrible PPO

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:23.070
thing that no one
but DeepSeek can run,

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:25.950
then that is much less of
an impact, at least for us

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:29.010
researchers than this
kind of GRPO thing,

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:31.550
which anyone can really
play with, even you

00:27:31.550 --> 00:27:32.612
in your assignments.

00:27:32.612 --> 00:27:35.070
And finally, they have some
really interesting distillation

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:40.750
insights that I think
have held up even now.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:43.510
So the starting point
of DeepSeek R1, and I

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.710
think you can see how a
lot of DeepSeek's expertise

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:47.690
build on each other.

00:27:47.690 --> 00:27:49.870
So they had this
DeepSeek math paper,

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:51.230
where they were doing GRPO.

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:54.630
They had already understood
a lot of the nuances of RL

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:56.430
on math problems.

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.470
And so because of
this, they started off

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.470
with a lot of the
components of DeepSeek math.

00:28:02.470 --> 00:28:05.230
But one of the really
important differences, and I'll

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.150
highlight an expert
just because I

00:28:07.150 --> 00:28:09.722
think it's particularly
important is they abandon

00:28:09.722 --> 00:28:12.180
process supervision, which was
the thing that worked really

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:14.420
well in DeepSeekMath,
and they go only

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:15.680
with outcome supervision.

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:17.440
And just to be clear,
outcome supervision

00:28:17.440 --> 00:28:19.940
is when you have reward only
for whether the final answer is

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:21.260
correct or incorrect.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:25.500
And process supervision is when
you have a grading rubric, let's

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:31.180
say, to check the validity of
intermediate steps in a proof.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:33.760
A lot of people thought process
supervision was important.

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:36.500
Turned out it wasn't
critical for a lot of things.

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:40.340
So R1 was interesting
in that it has

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:41.800
a lot of different components.

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:44.180
But one of the very
interesting components

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:47.020
is R1 zero, where
they don't really

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:50.020
do that much post training,
so they have a base model.

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:52.260
Of course, the base
model, as we all now

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:53.880
is also [? MIT ?] trained.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:57.380
And so this guy can do some
amount of instruction following.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.560
And then on top of
this base model,

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:04.220
they do basically RLVR, where
the rewards for their GRPO

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:07.780
algorithm is accuracy, rewards
for whether they correctly

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:11.540
solve a collection of math
problems and format rewards.

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:14.000
And this one is important
because the format rewards

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:16.980
basically allow them to strip
out the chain of thought later.

00:29:16.980 --> 00:29:18.840
They want the model
to properly use

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:21.920
thinking tags to
enclose their COT.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.020
But this is a very
simple recipe.

00:29:24.020 --> 00:29:25.340
GRPO is very simple.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:27.400
Accuracy rewards
are very simple.

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:29.400
In fact, you will
implement and replicate

00:29:29.400 --> 00:29:32.360
essentially the set of
results in some ways,

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:34.220
and you do just this
very simple recipe.

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:35.220
And where do you end up?

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:38.520
Well, you end up something
that is only a little bit worse

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:40.100
than OpenAI O1.

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:43.105
So this is really,
really, really clean.

00:29:43.105 --> 00:29:44.480
And I like this
result because it

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:49.320
has none of the messes of a
real production post-training

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:50.180
pipeline thrown in.

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:52.580
You don't really
question whether oh,

00:29:52.580 --> 00:29:55.100
was it because of the RLHF
helping or this or that.

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:57.840
It's a very simple
base model plus GRPO.

00:29:57.840 --> 00:30:00.880
Your math abilities
are quite good.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.080
And I think R1 also
took off, partially

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.040
because of the
interesting phenomena

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.780
they claim to have
observed in the paper.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:09.780
So one of the things they
claim to have observed

00:30:09.780 --> 00:30:12.110
is as we go longer
and longer and longer

00:30:12.110 --> 00:30:14.653
during training, the model
is able to think for longer

00:30:14.653 --> 00:30:15.570
and longer and longer.

00:30:15.570 --> 00:30:17.950
Their COT get longer
and longer and longer.

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:20.590
And they also highlighted
this thing that I believe

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:24.110
went viral on social media,
which is like the aha moment,

00:30:24.110 --> 00:30:26.850
where the model is if
you train it for enough,

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:28.810
its COT is able
to do things like,

00:30:28.810 --> 00:30:31.990
oh, there's an aha moment
that I can flag here.

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:34.430
And they thought this
was really interesting.

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:37.350
It is unclear to me whether
either of these phenomena

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:41.330
are really particularly
impressive in many ways.

00:30:41.330 --> 00:30:45.630
We now know that longer CoTs
is arguably a natural side

00:30:45.630 --> 00:30:48.790
effect of the length
normalization of the GRPO

00:30:48.790 --> 00:30:49.630
algorithm.

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:54.110
And the aha moment
others have showed

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:58.810
is actually appears in
even the base model.

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:03.170
And so clearly, it can't just
be a result of the RL algorithm.

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:06.860
Lots of people say
aha, I can use this

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:08.720
as part of solving
the math problem.

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.900
The model has learned
it during pre-training.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:13.380
It's not surprising at
all that somehow during RL

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:16.100
just happens to get
extracted because it's

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:18.980
emitting a lot of math tokens.

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:21.260
So I think the phenomena
that was highlighted

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:24.720
is not particularly
salient or exciting,

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:29.140
but I do think R1 was a really
important milestone in that it

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:32.500
highlighted just how
simple RLVR could be.

00:31:32.500 --> 00:31:35.540
This is very, in some
sense clean as an algorithm

00:31:35.540 --> 00:31:38.900
and as a way of solving these
really difficult problems.

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:42.720
And so R1 zero was this very
nice, clean controlled setting.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:47.040
And then R1 was their attempt
to productionize this system.

00:31:47.040 --> 00:31:48.860
And I think one thing
that's nice about R1

00:31:48.860 --> 00:31:50.860
and I'll show you
the Qwen equivalent

00:31:50.860 --> 00:31:55.700
later, is you can see how you
stack all the pieces together.

00:31:55.700 --> 00:31:57.893
I think we've studied all
the pieces in isolation.

00:31:57.893 --> 00:31:59.620
We know where pre-training is.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:02.420
We know that there's
RLHF, we know SFT,

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.180
but how do we compose
all of the pieces

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:06.730
to actually get a
system at the end?

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:09.270
Well, usually the thing that
you do is something like,

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:10.920
well, we have our
mid-trained model,

00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:13.170
we're going to do a bunch
of reasoning training, maybe

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:15.390
some long context
extension somewhere too.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:17.230
And then we're going
to do RLHF at the end,

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:19.870
because that's in some sense,
the most user-facing object.

00:32:19.870 --> 00:32:22.450
We want to make sure that the
formatting and all of this

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:23.270
is nice.

00:32:23.270 --> 00:32:27.410
RLHF can very much
help with that process.

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:30.770
One of the other things
that they did was they

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:33.490
added a language consistency
reward for the chain of thought

00:32:33.490 --> 00:32:35.330
for their production model.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.110
I think if I remember
the report right,

00:32:37.110 --> 00:32:40.090
this was because
the R1 zero style

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:41.890
training without a
consistency reward

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:44.970
would language switch
in COT, and they

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:47.170
thought this was very
uninterpretable and slightly

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:48.050
disturbing.

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:49.850
And so they wanted to
just have it output

00:32:49.850 --> 00:32:51.590
a single consistent language.

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:56.930
And so they do this just for
interpretability reasons.

00:32:56.930 --> 00:32:59.290
And then they have some of
the non-verifiable rewards

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:02.570
as well, thrown
in to GRPO, almost

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:05.020
as blending into
the RLHF process,

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:07.580
but otherwise it's a
very, very similar thing.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:12.840
And the whole pipeline, I
think, is very straightforward.

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:17.420
So for SFT, what they
do is pretty simple.

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:21.180
So in R1 zero, they don't
want to do any SFT at all.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:24.220
But in R1, they're willing
to do long COT data.

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:26.580
And when you're reading these
open source tech reports,

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:28.690
it's fun to try to
read between the lines.

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:30.440
When they say something
like, we construct

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:32.810
and collect a small
amount of long COT data,

00:33:32.810 --> 00:33:33.560
you might wonder--

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:36.660
I wonder if that was distilled
from some other models.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:40.440
That is a particularly
like negative thing

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:42.320
for open source models.

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:45.240
Everyone is nowadays doing
this, but I just find it funny

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:48.360
that it's very carefully
written collect a small amount

00:33:48.360 --> 00:33:50.120
of long COT data.

00:33:50.120 --> 00:33:52.460
And so this is used to
fine tune the model.

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:56.400
We know that for a very good
base model, just SFT on long COT

00:33:56.400 --> 00:34:00.120
can unlock a lot of
O1 style capabilities.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:02.850
And that's a great
starting point for RL.

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:05.870
So they start there and then
they add some verification

00:34:05.870 --> 00:34:08.030
to filter out the CoTs.

00:34:08.030 --> 00:34:13.030
And then they continue
on with processing.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:16.030
And I think many
papers since then,

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:18.150
some including some
from my students

00:34:18.150 --> 00:34:20.110
have shown things
that if you have

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:21.830
the right kind of
distillation procedure

00:34:21.830 --> 00:34:23.770
and you have the right
kinds of base model,

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:28.030
you can basically get a lot of
the long COT reasoning juice

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:29.350
just from SFT.

00:34:29.350 --> 00:34:31.350
And I think this is
maybe interesting

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:33.030
because one of the
things that I think

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:35.830
is still an open question
is, do you really

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:37.770
need RL for some of this.

00:34:37.770 --> 00:34:40.350
And I think maybe one way of
thinking about the role of RL

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:42.350
in language modeling
is that RL is

00:34:42.350 --> 00:34:43.830
a great source of supervision.

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:45.852
If you're solving
frontier math problems,

00:34:45.852 --> 00:34:47.310
you just don't have
the supervision

00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:49.630
to get detailed long
CoTs, and RL allows

00:34:49.630 --> 00:34:51.050
you to self-generate that.

00:34:51.050 --> 00:34:53.530
But once someone has
generated these long CoTs,

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:56.449
you could potentially
also learn from imitation.

00:34:56.449 --> 00:34:59.980
And that seems to be what a lot
of these distillation results

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:03.180
partially show.

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:07.340
So the RL part for R1 is
basically the same as R1 zero.

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:10.120
Minus the language consistency
loss that I mentioned,

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:14.140
it's basically just we run
the RL training using GRPO.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:18.380
And that leads to
essentially the final phase,

00:35:18.380 --> 00:35:21.000
which is what we learned
about last lecture.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:25.820
So we do some basic instruction
tuning style SFT and then RLHF.

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:29.380
For non-verifiable tasks, they
basically use the same thing

00:35:29.380 --> 00:35:30.840
as DeepSeek V3.

00:35:30.840 --> 00:35:34.300
So there's really no
surprises at all here.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:35.460
How well does it work?

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:37.940
It's really, really good.

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:40.180
And I think this is the
reason why there was all

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:45.460
that panic or big
noticing of DeepSeek

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:48.380
because R1 was genuinely
a very good model.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:52.297
It beat O1 on many
of the categories.

00:35:52.297 --> 00:35:54.380
It matched a lot of the
test time scaling behavior

00:35:54.380 --> 00:35:55.900
that people expected.

00:35:55.900 --> 00:35:58.090
And it came from a
very simple recipe.

00:35:58.090 --> 00:36:00.290
So it was very easy
to understand where

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:04.160
the gains were coming from.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.410
And the last thing related
to some of the distillation

00:36:06.410 --> 00:36:11.650
discussions that I was saying
is you can then take R1's CoTs

00:36:11.650 --> 00:36:14.210
and you could put
it into Qwen 2.5,

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:17.530
and if you did so, you could
really significantly boost

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:19.030
the performance of these models.

00:36:19.030 --> 00:36:22.850
In some cases, matching a lot
of these specialized thinking

00:36:22.850 --> 00:36:23.790
models.

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:26.050
And so in other
words, really, if you

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:28.650
can get the right
kinds of long CoTs

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:30.890
that are legible
to these models,

00:36:30.890 --> 00:36:33.490
often it is possible
to get them to reason

00:36:33.490 --> 00:36:36.810
for long periods of time, like
the base models are already

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:40.090
surprisingly good, and this
works even for Llama models

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:45.070
as well, which I think
is quite fascinating.

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:50.890
So to close out
the DeepSeek saga,

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:53.510
I quite liked a lot of
the DeepSeek tech reports,

00:36:53.510 --> 00:36:56.330
because they often
do both ablations

00:36:56.330 --> 00:36:59.680
and they also tell you failed
things that didn't work.

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:02.360
And if you've been following
what they've been doing,

00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:05.380
DeepSeekMath has all of this
process reward model stuff.

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:07.280
It's like we got to
verify all the steps

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:10.000
and then you read R1 and you
say where did the process reward

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:10.540
models go.

00:37:10.540 --> 00:37:13.220
Well, they tell you where
the process reward models go,

00:37:13.220 --> 00:37:16.900
which is we tried to get
process reward models to work.

00:37:16.900 --> 00:37:19.380
They just didn't do
very much for us.

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:23.040
It turns out that outcome
reward models are great

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:24.260
and they're good enough.

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:26.960
And you can scale the data
for those a lot better.

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:29.380
I think a big
debate for PRMs was,

00:37:29.380 --> 00:37:32.200
where are you going to get
these step-by-step rubrics.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.400
And it's very hard
to scale that up.

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:36.240
I think at this point,
it's quite clear now

00:37:36.240 --> 00:37:38.940
that outcome reward models
are very, very good.

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:42.240
And that's the bulk of where
the action is happening.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:47.420
Similarly, if you were around
for the initial O1 release,

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:50.440
a lot of people speculated
about what was going on

00:37:50.440 --> 00:37:52.420
inside OpenAI's O1.

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:54.400
They were like, oh,
are they doing PRMs?

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:57.550
Are they doing tree
search like AlphaGo?

00:37:57.550 --> 00:38:00.670
They to tried a lot
of MCTS and they

00:38:00.670 --> 00:38:04.270
describe we couldn't get
it to work very well.

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:06.070
I just very much
want to highlight

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:08.990
and the fact that they're
very open about all

00:38:08.990 --> 00:38:11.110
these explorations and
the things that they tried

00:38:11.110 --> 00:38:15.670
and didn't work, rather than to
say they didn't try it at all.

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:17.550
Cool.

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:18.910
All right.

00:38:18.910 --> 00:38:21.478
And if I talk about--

00:38:21.478 --> 00:38:23.270
or actually, I'll pause
here in case anyone

00:38:23.270 --> 00:38:25.370
has questions, although
I don't know if-- yeah.

00:38:25.370 --> 00:38:30.510
[INAUDIBLE] you should explain
the positive reward basis

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:36.830
by [INAUDIBLE] Because I
thought you said for negative,

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:40.510
like we encourage to divide
by infinity and [INAUDIBLE] 0

00:38:40.510 --> 00:38:41.370
for positive.

00:38:41.370 --> 00:38:41.870
Yeah.

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:43.750
So the question was
like for positive cases

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:45.463
what the length normalizer do.

00:38:45.463 --> 00:38:47.630
And of course, in those
cases, the length normalizer

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:50.525
encourages you to shorten
the COT, which is good

00:38:50.525 --> 00:38:52.150
if you want to save
on inference costs,

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:55.140
bad if that degrades
your accuracy.

00:38:55.140 --> 00:38:58.420
But I think the main issue
is that for the long--

00:38:58.420 --> 00:39:01.950
for the negative ones, you
get some really long responses

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:03.700
and the positive cases,
it can't shrink it

00:39:03.700 --> 00:39:05.867
that much because there's
a lower bound to how small

00:39:05.867 --> 00:39:09.820
your COT can go to solve
a particular problem.

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:13.660
I guess I'm trying to
understand that explanation

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:16.480
for the diagram.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:16.980
Is that--

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:21.160
I think doctor GRPO has a
cleaner diagram of this.

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:24.600
So in the R1 diagram,
this is all aggregated.

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:28.400
This is all of the responses
across their evals.

00:39:28.400 --> 00:39:30.660
It's not just the positive ones.

00:39:30.660 --> 00:39:36.940
But if we look at the
doctor GRPO plot over here.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:39.980
So this is like the average
output length and this

00:39:39.980 --> 00:39:41.720
is the incorrect output length.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:45.000
And so you see that in the
correct output length is here.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:49.100
So you see somewhat
that this is really

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:50.660
being driven by
the incorrect ones

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:52.910
as we would expect
from the explanation.

00:39:52.910 --> 00:39:56.470
So the story, at least in these
sets of controlled comparisons,

00:39:56.470 --> 00:39:58.250
is quite clean and clear.

00:39:58.250 --> 00:40:00.250
Yeah.

00:40:00.250 --> 00:40:02.650
Cool.

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:06.610
So now, I'm also going to talk
about an alternative method,

00:40:06.610 --> 00:40:11.210
Kimi K1.5 or alternative
paper and approach.

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:14.450
And I always feel like
when I talk about R1,

00:40:14.450 --> 00:40:18.810
I'm obligated to talk about
Kimi K1.5 because they came out

00:40:18.810 --> 00:40:20.570
at the same time as R1.

00:40:20.570 --> 00:40:24.910
They also beat R1, and yet
everyone is all about DeepSeek,

00:40:24.910 --> 00:40:29.030
and Kimi is not quite as often
brought up in the conversation,

00:40:29.030 --> 00:40:31.490
even though their models
are extremely good.

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:36.210
And I'm not just talking about
them out of pity, per se.

00:40:36.210 --> 00:40:39.070
I think Kimi has this nice
thing that they do something--

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:42.530
some sets of things, quite
differently than DeepSeek,

00:40:42.530 --> 00:40:45.530
and we can learn quite a
bit from the fact that both

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:46.270
of these work.

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:48.730
I think this is part of the
theme of this class, which

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:51.040
is by reading all of
these tech reports

00:40:51.040 --> 00:40:52.500
and by looking at
broader patterns,

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:54.280
we can roughly
start to understand

00:40:54.280 --> 00:40:58.480
what are the valid
or easy spaces to get

00:40:58.480 --> 00:41:00.000
these algorithms to work.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:02.320
So they do actually
quite a bit more

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:05.480
detail in talking about the data
set construction and curriculum

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:08.440
generation for the RL
process, which many have said

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:10.120
is quite important.

00:41:10.120 --> 00:41:12.260
And they also have a
different RL algorithm,

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:15.080
which in some ways,
has similar intuitions

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:16.980
but is not exactly GRPO.

00:41:16.980 --> 00:41:20.320
And so this is another nice
validation that technically

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:23.000
speaking, I don't
think you necessarily

00:41:23.000 --> 00:41:29.600
need something like GRPO
to get these guys to work.

00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:33.560
So the first part I want
to talk about is the data.

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:37.680
The data remains very important
for all of these processes.

00:41:37.680 --> 00:41:40.000
And for RL, there's
an additional wrinkle

00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:43.440
of curriculum, what
difficulties of problems

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:44.680
do you want to throw in?

00:41:44.680 --> 00:41:47.280
In SFT, you don't really
think about difficulty

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:49.790
very much because you just
jam the data in there.

00:41:49.790 --> 00:41:51.710
For whatever data that
you want, you just

00:41:51.710 --> 00:41:53.310
do pre-training
losses and the model

00:41:53.310 --> 00:41:54.730
is forced to learn from these.

00:41:54.730 --> 00:41:56.730
In SFT, there's a little
bit more of a subtlety.

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:59.173
We talked quite a bit about
the hallucination issues,

00:41:59.173 --> 00:42:00.590
and maybe sometimes
you don't want

00:42:00.590 --> 00:42:02.830
to train on some of this data.

00:42:02.830 --> 00:42:05.910
But for RL, there's
this additional wrinkle

00:42:05.910 --> 00:42:08.853
that if your problems are
too hard, you get no rewards.

00:42:08.853 --> 00:42:10.770
And if you get no rewards,
you have no signal.

00:42:10.770 --> 00:42:12.710
And if you have no
signal, you can't learn.

00:42:12.710 --> 00:42:15.950
So it's very important
to have broad coverage

00:42:15.950 --> 00:42:19.510
of the right kinds of examples.

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:23.130
And so what the Kimi
folks do is first of all,

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:26.490
they try to get a whole big,
broad range of coverage of data.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:29.230
Unsurprising, they
exclude multiple choice

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:31.690
because that's covered in
lots of different domains.

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:33.838
They want things that
require long, deep thought.

00:42:33.838 --> 00:42:35.630
And their argument is
maybe multiple choice

00:42:35.630 --> 00:42:36.730
doesn't have that.

00:42:36.730 --> 00:42:38.870
But maybe this last
one I think is maybe

00:42:38.870 --> 00:42:40.090
the most important idea.

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:43.870
This appears in a lot
of different RL papers.

00:42:43.870 --> 00:42:46.860
You basically filter all
the examples for things

00:42:46.860 --> 00:42:50.780
that either pass or
sorry, you filter them

00:42:50.780 --> 00:42:52.800
based on a best of k filter.

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:55.740
So here you take examples.

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:58.560
And if they succeed
on best of 8,

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:00.700
if you sample eight
times and the model

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:04.600
can do it at least once, then
that's not a great RL example,

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:07.600
because it's already at the edge
of the model's capabilities.

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:10.220
You're not going to teach it
anything particularly new.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:16.020
And so you only look at examples
that basically fail this test,

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:19.980
because that allows
us to essentially pick

00:43:19.980 --> 00:43:22.400
the right kinds of problems.

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:24.340
And so this saves compute.

00:43:24.340 --> 00:43:26.740
It allows you to skip
problems entirely.

00:43:26.740 --> 00:43:29.100
You can also filter
on both sides

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:31.820
to get problems that are
neither too hard nor too easy.

00:43:31.820 --> 00:43:34.260
I think the general consensus
in the research community

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:37.900
is that doing this medium
range difficulty filtering

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:39.940
is very good if
really what you want

00:43:39.940 --> 00:43:44.500
is for RL to progress
at a steady pace.

00:43:44.500 --> 00:43:47.190
As with DeepSeek no
description about SFT.

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:49.170
We can speculate
about what that is,

00:43:49.170 --> 00:43:53.098
but we have no
concrete information.

00:43:53.098 --> 00:43:54.890
So I think the interesting
thing about Kimi

00:43:54.890 --> 00:43:58.660
is that they have a
DPO inspired argument,

00:43:58.660 --> 00:44:00.410
but they end up actually
in a place that's

00:44:00.410 --> 00:44:03.010
very similar as GRPO.

00:44:03.010 --> 00:44:05.690
And I think the fact that
they end up in similar places

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:08.250
is suggestive of which
of these components

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:12.470
are potentially useful if you're
designing a new RL algorithm,

00:44:12.470 --> 00:44:13.170
for example.

00:44:13.170 --> 00:44:15.970
So they start at the exact
same place as everyone else.

00:44:15.970 --> 00:44:19.190
So this should now look
very familiar to all of you.

00:44:19.190 --> 00:44:22.790
It's just maximize the expected
reward under my policy.

00:44:22.790 --> 00:44:25.330
And I've got this KL
regularizer on the right that

00:44:25.330 --> 00:44:29.250
keeps me close to my
base policy, or maybe

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:30.910
even the previous
iterations policy,

00:44:30.910 --> 00:44:34.450
depending on how you're going
to do this-- do this RL.

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:38.170
So they're going to follow the
same DPO style derivation, where

00:44:38.170 --> 00:44:42.010
they say assume that I
can maximize analytically

00:44:42.010 --> 00:44:43.920
and I solve for the
reward model that

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:46.040
does that maximization
or corresponding

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:47.300
to that maximization.

00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:51.360
And that's going to give
me this ratio of policies.

00:44:51.360 --> 00:44:54.800
And I'm going to plug that back
in to my objective up there

00:44:54.800 --> 00:44:58.520
and try to solve for
what is happening.

00:44:58.520 --> 00:45:00.920
Well, what they're
now saying is they

00:45:00.920 --> 00:45:04.320
want to try to match
the reward model.

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:05.800
And for this
equality to be-- they

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:09.220
want this equality to be true,
which is true at the minimizer.

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:11.680
And therefore, this
is a big heuristic.

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:13.700
If something's equal
at the minimizer,

00:45:13.700 --> 00:45:15.960
why don't we just put a
squared loss on that thing

00:45:15.960 --> 00:45:18.240
and minimize the squared loss?

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:20.120
I think optimization
people looking at this

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:22.127
would be horrified
at this, but I

00:45:22.127 --> 00:45:23.960
think this is a totally
reasonable intuition

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:24.502
in some ways.

00:45:24.502 --> 00:45:26.640
We know that these two
things should be close

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:28.020
when the model is good.

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:30.760
So it is also maybe OK
to treat making those two

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:34.800
things explicitly
close as a surrogate.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:38.520
This derivation, as you can
see, is very, very different

00:45:38.520 --> 00:45:39.700
from GRPO.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:45.030
We're not going through the
PPO path to derive this update.

00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:46.810
But now, actually,
lo and behold,

00:45:46.810 --> 00:45:48.870
you take a gradient
of this L, you

00:45:48.870 --> 00:45:53.410
end up with something that
looks surprisingly like GRPO

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:55.830
with an added regularizer term.

00:45:55.830 --> 00:45:58.590
What you end up
with is this thing

00:45:58.590 --> 00:46:02.870
that is a policy gradient
with a baseline R bar.

00:46:02.870 --> 00:46:07.450
And that R bar is actually
the mean of each condition,

00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:08.830
these x's.

00:46:08.830 --> 00:46:11.810
And you have this
additional regularizer term,

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:15.650
which is essentially the KL
term that would appear in GRPO.

00:46:15.650 --> 00:46:18.270
So in some sense,
we've reinvented

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:21.070
the group mean
normalized baseline

00:46:21.070 --> 00:46:23.250
through quite different means.

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:31.590
And so the Kimi folks
I think in some ways

00:46:31.590 --> 00:46:36.130
had a nicer or better view
of the length problem.

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:37.770
I think the GRPO
folks were like,

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:41.080
isn't it great that the length
is growing uncontrollably.

00:46:41.080 --> 00:46:42.647
I'm sure our model
is being smarter.

00:46:42.647 --> 00:46:44.480
That's a little bit of
an uncharitable take,

00:46:44.480 --> 00:46:47.232
but when you present this
plot as a positive thing,

00:46:47.232 --> 00:46:48.940
the implied statement
is like, it's great

00:46:48.940 --> 00:46:51.540
that our model is
thinking for longer.

00:46:51.540 --> 00:46:55.660
But the Kimi folks look
at this long COT problem

00:46:55.660 --> 00:47:00.840
and they say, well, if we're
making long CoTs potentially,

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:02.060
that's very wasteful.

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:06.100
We don't really want long CoTs
because CoTs cost inference.

00:47:06.100 --> 00:47:10.217
So not only do they not
have a length problem,

00:47:10.217 --> 00:47:11.800
because if you look
at this objective,

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:14.140
they don't normalize
by sequence length,

00:47:14.140 --> 00:47:16.660
but they actually want
to go even further

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:19.160
and they want to compress
the length of the response.

00:47:19.160 --> 00:47:21.940
And this is another
important feature

00:47:21.940 --> 00:47:26.120
that we see generally occurring
in language model development.

00:47:26.120 --> 00:47:28.560
We want the CoTs to be
as short as possible.

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:30.040
We want to solve hard problems.

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:31.900
We don't want to
think for very long,

00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:33.400
because when we
think for very long,

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:36.400
we have to essentially
subsidize the user.

00:47:36.400 --> 00:47:41.173
If you're OpenAI and your
users have the $200 Pro Plan,

00:47:41.173 --> 00:47:42.840
and your models are
thinking for an hour

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:45.102
at a time, that's not a
very good place to be in.

00:47:45.102 --> 00:47:46.560
Whereas if your
models are thinking

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:49.880
for five minutes at a time,
that is a great place to be in.

00:47:49.880 --> 00:47:52.400
And so for all of
these reasons, Kimi

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:54.600
wants to go and
compress the CoTs.

00:47:54.600 --> 00:47:55.820
How do you do that?

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:58.360
Well, the simplest
are all thing to do

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:02.320
is you come up with a
heuristic length reward

00:48:02.320 --> 00:48:07.200
and you just tack it
on to the RL process.

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:11.720
This reward is
surprisingly complicated.

00:48:11.720 --> 00:48:14.480
And the thing that
they're doing is

00:48:14.480 --> 00:48:18.480
you're trying to make longer
sequences shorter and shorter.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:22.120
Correct answers also
should be short.

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:24.160
The interesting
balance here is if you

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:26.260
make the incorrect
answers too short,

00:48:26.260 --> 00:48:28.230
they no longer have
a way to recover.

00:48:28.230 --> 00:48:29.480
So let me give you an example.

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:32.800
Imagine I'm bad at geometry.

00:48:32.800 --> 00:48:36.810
AI bad at geometry, I get
lots of incorrect answers,

00:48:36.810 --> 00:48:39.890
and the penalty makes my
geometry CoTs really short.

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:42.210
Now, my geometry CoTs are 0.

00:48:42.210 --> 00:48:43.770
I'm really bad at geometry.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:45.070
I will never recover from this.

00:48:45.070 --> 00:48:47.850
I will never get a positive
geometry reward ever again.

00:48:47.850 --> 00:48:48.790
And I'm stuck.

00:48:48.790 --> 00:48:52.070
And so what you do is you don't
force the incorrect answers

00:48:52.070 --> 00:48:53.870
to be super short.

00:48:53.870 --> 00:48:56.310
You incentivize them to be
just a little bit shorter

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:58.910
than the average, so
that the incorrect stuff

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:02.190
doesn't grow unboundedly.

00:49:02.190 --> 00:49:07.270
And so this has, in the end,
a surprisingly reasonable set

00:49:07.270 --> 00:49:10.190
of outcomes.

00:49:10.190 --> 00:49:15.150
And so as I said before, they
have a really nice RL set

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:18.290
of views on how they do
their data set curation.

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:21.010
So not only do they have this
data set that they filter,

00:49:21.010 --> 00:49:22.990
they also look at
the success rates

00:49:22.990 --> 00:49:25.930
of how often they're able to
solve particular problems.

00:49:25.930 --> 00:49:28.090
And once the model masters
a particular problem,

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:30.010
it gets taken out
of the problem set.

00:49:30.010 --> 00:49:31.770
So you save some
compute doing that.

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:35.940
I think basically everyone
doing RL does these success rate

00:49:35.940 --> 00:49:38.180
filtering to avoid
both wasting compute

00:49:38.180 --> 00:49:43.380
or working on problems that
are way too hard for the model.

00:49:43.380 --> 00:49:47.722
And they basically have
rewards that, for code, they

00:49:47.722 --> 00:49:50.180
take ground truth solutions
and they generate some new test

00:49:50.180 --> 00:49:51.000
cases.

00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:53.740
And for math, they actually
have a reward model

00:49:53.740 --> 00:49:55.900
to check for answer
equivalence checks.

00:49:55.900 --> 00:49:58.400
And this is a really
funny thing in some ways,

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:00.180
because we started
out this lecture

00:50:00.180 --> 00:50:04.160
by saying we want to work
on formal math or something,

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.180
something truly
verifiable, where you can--

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:09.460
a compiler can check the
correctness of your math.

00:50:09.460 --> 00:50:12.160
And then we've gone through
most of the lecture.

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:14.320
And then in the end,
where we ended up?

00:50:14.320 --> 00:50:16.480
Well, we ended up
with a reward model,

00:50:16.480 --> 00:50:20.580
a reward model that checks the
correctness of math answers.

00:50:20.580 --> 00:50:24.600
Once again, you'll see
why in the assignment

00:50:24.600 --> 00:50:27.540
or maybe if you play with the
assignment, you'll see why.

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:30.820
You'll quickly find out that
answer equivalence checking

00:50:30.820 --> 00:50:32.700
is a very difficult problem.

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:36.910
Because in math, you can write
equivalent things in many ways.

00:50:36.910 --> 00:50:38.370
And not only that,
a language model

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:40.550
can give the answer
back in many ways.

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:43.130
Even if you prompt it to
say give the answer back

00:50:43.130 --> 00:50:46.750
in latex boxed, maybe
sometimes it skips the boxed,

00:50:46.750 --> 00:50:49.010
maybe add some extra
stuff to the box.

00:50:49.010 --> 00:50:51.530
There are lots of ways
that the model can just

00:50:51.530 --> 00:50:55.350
fail to get the right answer by
a strict correctness checker,

00:50:55.350 --> 00:50:57.090
even though it has
the right idea.

00:50:57.090 --> 00:51:03.090
And so for these reasons,
basically, most RL projects

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:06.810
have a very complicated
answer checker, either a regex

00:51:06.810 --> 00:51:08.850
or a model, or who
knows what in order

00:51:08.850 --> 00:51:10.470
to avoid these
kinds of problems.

00:51:10.470 --> 00:51:18.570
It's a real rabbit hole getting
the verified part of RLVR.

00:51:18.570 --> 00:51:20.570
Cool.

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:25.010
The last thing which connects
to some of the systems bits

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:27.650
that we had talked about
earlier and the inference bits

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:30.190
is RL optimization.

00:51:30.190 --> 00:51:33.680
And RL infra is
really quite hard.

00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:36.260
Training is hard and
inference is hard.

00:51:36.260 --> 00:51:37.860
And RL puts the two together.

00:51:37.860 --> 00:51:42.400
So in some ways, no wonder is it
really horrible and difficult.

00:51:42.400 --> 00:51:45.360
Even worse, I think one of the
things that you don't initially

00:51:45.360 --> 00:51:49.320
appreciate about how hard
it is to make RL efficient

00:51:49.320 --> 00:51:52.080
is think of the
following scenario.

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:54.660
Now, imagine you've
got your rollouts,

00:51:54.660 --> 00:51:56.640
but you've got one
really hard math problem,

00:51:56.640 --> 00:51:59.340
let's say one of them is
like the Riemann hypothesis.

00:51:59.340 --> 00:52:01.120
And your model is
really chugging along

00:52:01.120 --> 00:52:02.260
on the Riemann hypothesis.

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:04.480
It's got this gigantic COT.

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:05.498
Now, what's happening?

00:52:05.498 --> 00:52:07.540
In the meantime, if you're
doing naive inference,

00:52:07.540 --> 00:52:09.373
everyone else is waiting
on this one rollout

00:52:09.373 --> 00:52:12.340
to complete in order to
move on to the next phase.

00:52:12.340 --> 00:52:14.440
So if you're doing
these batch things,

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:17.360
long rollouts can
really hurt you.

00:52:17.360 --> 00:52:22.280
And so RL really needs to
deal with all of these really

00:52:22.280 --> 00:52:24.065
tricky low-level details.

00:52:24.065 --> 00:52:25.440
So you need to
deal with the fact

00:52:25.440 --> 00:52:27.820
that some CoTs can
get really long.

00:52:27.820 --> 00:52:30.390
Do you need to truncate
them, do you somehow

00:52:30.390 --> 00:52:32.430
set them into a different
machine, who knows?

00:52:32.430 --> 00:52:34.950
These are all decisions
that you can make.

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:37.710
If you switch from training
to rollouts, you roll out once

00:52:37.710 --> 00:52:39.930
and then you train and you
roll out and you train,

00:52:39.930 --> 00:52:42.090
you have to somehow
deal with this.

00:52:42.090 --> 00:52:44.410
Either some of your machines
are pure rollout machines,

00:52:44.410 --> 00:52:47.210
and some of them are
training machines,

00:52:47.210 --> 00:52:49.370
or you're switching out
frameworks all the time.

00:52:49.370 --> 00:52:51.630
Both of them are very costly.

00:52:51.630 --> 00:52:54.710
And then finally, you have this
really difficult and horrible

00:52:54.710 --> 00:52:58.350
trade off, which is on policy
stuff is mathematically

00:52:58.350 --> 00:53:00.710
and training dynamics
wise very nice.

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:05.490
So GRPO in its simple on policy
form behaves very nicely.

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:07.790
You will experience
this in your assignments

00:53:07.790 --> 00:53:09.770
and then you will get greedy.

00:53:09.770 --> 00:53:13.490
You will say but my systems
utilization is so low.

00:53:13.490 --> 00:53:17.130
I could do so much better if
only I could reuse my rollouts,

00:53:17.130 --> 00:53:19.590
then I can overlap my
inference and computation

00:53:19.590 --> 00:53:21.330
and do all clever things.

00:53:21.330 --> 00:53:23.910
And so you will attempt
to reuse these rollouts.

00:53:23.910 --> 00:53:26.270
And that will lead to
off-policy problems which

00:53:26.270 --> 00:53:28.900
then lead to destabilizing
your training

00:53:28.900 --> 00:53:31.260
and all sorts of things
that are very difficult.

00:53:31.260 --> 00:53:34.180
So these are all sorts
of things that end up

00:53:34.180 --> 00:53:37.700
getting very, very tricky.

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:41.380
And so I think most open
source technical reports will

00:53:41.380 --> 00:53:46.100
have these days a section
talking about their RL infra,

00:53:46.100 --> 00:53:48.460
and their RL infra
is going to have

00:53:48.460 --> 00:53:50.360
to have both a training part.

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:53.260
That's the blue boxes over here
and an inference part, which

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:55.340
is the green part on the right.

00:53:55.340 --> 00:53:58.260
And you're going to have to move
weights from the training part

00:53:58.260 --> 00:53:59.200
to the inference part.

00:53:59.200 --> 00:54:02.540
So you need some of story
for how to move those around.

00:54:02.540 --> 00:54:05.100
And you need to
coordinate them closely.

00:54:05.100 --> 00:54:06.980
And in some cases,
maybe they even

00:54:06.980 --> 00:54:09.980
share the same machines because
as inference is running,

00:54:09.980 --> 00:54:11.660
the training one might be idle.

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:13.400
So all of these
are complexities.

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:17.220
And you have lots of very
complicated systems things

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:19.620
to do.

00:54:19.620 --> 00:54:25.060
So we already know that
Kimi beats OpenAI O1,

00:54:25.060 --> 00:54:27.790
but we also see some
really nice things

00:54:27.790 --> 00:54:31.290
showing that as RL proceeds,
you're able to think longer

00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:32.830
and performance goes up.

00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:34.890
But in some cases, it's
not that we're just

00:54:34.890 --> 00:54:36.930
unboundedly increasing tokens.

00:54:36.930 --> 00:54:38.570
We end up getting
cases where I think

00:54:38.570 --> 00:54:40.150
OmniMath is a good example.

00:54:40.150 --> 00:54:41.950
You're not thinking
for that much longer,

00:54:41.950 --> 00:54:43.630
but your performance
continues to go up.

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:47.890
So maybe this is a good example
of this length control kicking

00:54:47.890 --> 00:54:51.410
in and doing good things.

00:54:51.410 --> 00:54:55.130
The final thing I want to talk
about is you might wonder,

00:54:55.130 --> 00:54:57.570
is this actual RL
stuff much better

00:54:57.570 --> 00:55:00.450
than just training
on correct answers.

00:55:00.450 --> 00:55:02.270
This is called expert iteration.

00:55:02.270 --> 00:55:05.450
It worked very well in
a number of past papers.

00:55:05.450 --> 00:55:07.750
In cases where you're dealing
with very unstable stuff,

00:55:07.750 --> 00:55:10.930
you may actually want to do
expert iteration instead.

00:55:10.930 --> 00:55:13.650
Kimi K1 has very
large scale ablations

00:55:13.650 --> 00:55:17.650
showing that these
kinds of RL methods

00:55:17.650 --> 00:55:20.110
work consistently better
than expert iteration.

00:55:20.110 --> 00:55:22.910
So that's the orange
beating the blue over here.

00:55:22.910 --> 00:55:26.600
So you can't really avoid RL
if you want to squeeze out

00:55:26.600 --> 00:55:29.380
all of your performance.

00:55:29.380 --> 00:55:31.440
I can pause here
in case anyone has

00:55:31.440 --> 00:55:32.820
questions about the Kimi stuff.

00:55:32.820 --> 00:55:35.880
And then I'll move on
to the last of the three

00:55:35.880 --> 00:55:37.140
which is Qwen 3.

00:55:40.140 --> 00:55:40.640
Great.

00:55:40.640 --> 00:55:42.480
OK.

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:46.360
So the last one that I want to
talk about is Qwen 3 and also

00:55:46.360 --> 00:55:48.940
Qwen 3.5-Next-Coder.

00:55:48.940 --> 00:55:51.680
The names are
getting complicated.

00:55:51.680 --> 00:55:53.300
And Qwen 3 is later.

00:55:53.300 --> 00:55:56.000
But they have actually really
interesting scaling and data

00:55:56.000 --> 00:55:56.740
results.

00:55:56.740 --> 00:55:58.440
So I want to talk about those.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:02.520
And the successor to this,
which is Qwen 3.5-Next-Coder is

00:56:02.520 --> 00:56:06.400
interesting because I think has
the most details of I would say

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:12.320
agentic RLVR training out of the
major tech reports and they do

00:56:12.320 --> 00:56:14.760
lots of cool things.

00:56:14.760 --> 00:56:18.600
So once again, as
with DeepSeek, one

00:56:18.600 --> 00:56:21.200
of the nice things that we
see with the Qwen report

00:56:21.200 --> 00:56:24.250
is the full way that all
of these are organized.

00:56:24.250 --> 00:56:27.630
And this is very similar
to the DeepSeek structure.

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:32.990
We do base model to basically
SFT, then reasoning RL.

00:56:32.990 --> 00:56:35.410
And then they have this
thinking mode fusion.

00:56:35.410 --> 00:56:37.290
I'll talk about that later.

00:56:37.290 --> 00:56:38.870
And then they do
RLHF and then they

00:56:38.870 --> 00:56:40.240
have a model that gets shipped.

00:56:40.240 --> 00:56:42.490
But of course, they don't
want to serve that directly.

00:56:42.490 --> 00:56:45.890
So they do distillation to
get their smaller models.

00:56:45.890 --> 00:56:50.450
I mean, Qwen 3 is quite a
respectable open source model.

00:56:50.450 --> 00:56:53.950
You can basically have this
be your mental picture of how

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:58.590
frontier-ish language
models are built putting

00:56:58.590 --> 00:56:59.850
all the components together.

00:56:59.850 --> 00:57:02.100
And I think you would have
a pretty reasonable picture

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:03.050
of what is happening.

00:57:05.840 --> 00:57:09.230
And Qwen uses basically, at
this point, tried and tested

00:57:09.230 --> 00:57:11.590
playbook for RLVR.

00:57:11.590 --> 00:57:14.890
I think they take lots of the
best parts of Kimi and DeepSeek,

00:57:14.890 --> 00:57:16.289
and they get it right.

00:57:16.289 --> 00:57:18.456
They do a lot of filtering
for difficulty because we

00:57:18.456 --> 00:57:19.770
know that saves compute.

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:22.540
That's a good way
to get good data.

00:57:22.540 --> 00:57:26.060
They also do other things,
like they remove things

00:57:26.060 --> 00:57:27.860
that the model can
get right without COT

00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:30.340
because we know that's
not a thinking problem.

00:57:30.340 --> 00:57:33.100
They remove things that are
too similar to validation data

00:57:33.100 --> 00:57:35.580
to try to decontaminate things.

00:57:35.580 --> 00:57:38.980
And then they do a little
bit of manual filtering

00:57:38.980 --> 00:57:41.820
on reference CoTs.

00:57:41.820 --> 00:57:46.180
Maybe most remarkably about Qwen
3 is they actually do their RL

00:57:46.180 --> 00:57:50.180
on very few examples,
just 4,000 examples.

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:53.440
But once again, if you have
the rest of the pipeline right,

00:57:53.440 --> 00:57:57.780
you can actually get
surprisingly far.

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:00.300
So the core of the
RLVR components

00:58:00.300 --> 00:58:04.100
are maybe not super surprising
because they are really

00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:08.580
building off of DeepSeek
R1 and also Kimi.

00:58:08.580 --> 00:58:12.860
There are some interesting Qwen
3 specific things, some of which

00:58:12.860 --> 00:58:15.500
they've ended up getting rid of,
but I think some of the ideas

00:58:15.500 --> 00:58:17.780
are actually quite interesting.

00:58:17.780 --> 00:58:20.250
So one of the
things that they do

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:24.330
is they mix thinking and
non-thinking things with tags.

00:58:24.330 --> 00:58:28.130
And that's how they end up
having a thinking mode, where

00:58:28.130 --> 00:58:30.530
the model has a long
COT and they can mix

00:58:30.530 --> 00:58:31.950
that with a non-thinking mode.

00:58:31.950 --> 00:58:35.315
So both the instant response
model and the long COT model

00:58:35.315 --> 00:58:36.690
basically live in
the same model.

00:58:36.690 --> 00:58:39.410
This was not true in many cases.

00:58:39.410 --> 00:58:42.610
There was often a thinking
mode and a non-thinking model,

00:58:42.610 --> 00:58:44.370
even at OpenAI.

00:58:44.370 --> 00:58:46.290
They also have--

00:58:46.290 --> 00:58:48.810
I thought this was
quite interesting,

00:58:48.810 --> 00:58:52.210
which is they have this way of
early exiting thinking where

00:58:52.210 --> 00:58:56.250
if they append a special string,
they'll immediately stop the COT

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:59.650
and the model is forced to give
an answer to whatever prompt

00:58:59.650 --> 00:59:00.890
is given.

00:59:00.890 --> 00:59:05.130
We see this even in-- we see
this affordance in interfaces

00:59:05.130 --> 00:59:08.570
like ChatGPT, where you're able
to terminate the thinking early

00:59:08.570 --> 00:59:11.650
and try to get an immediate
answer, for example.

00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:15.570
But what we see with
this and I've always

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:19.200
thought this was a surprising
an interesting thing, which

00:59:19.200 --> 00:59:22.320
is that as you vary the
thinking budget, doing

00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:25.000
this early termination
trick, you end up

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:28.920
finding that the
performance of the model

00:59:28.920 --> 00:59:32.800
degrades gracefully, even though
at the lower-thinking budgets,

00:59:32.800 --> 00:59:35.140
these models are kind of
getting truncated mid-thought,

00:59:35.140 --> 00:59:37.960
they're able to give
surprisingly reasonable

00:59:37.960 --> 00:59:40.620
responses even at that point.

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:42.120
And we see that
consistently, even

00:59:42.120 --> 00:59:44.240
with very small-thinking
budgets, the thinking

00:59:44.240 --> 00:59:46.120
mode models are
much better on all

00:59:46.120 --> 00:59:49.200
of these mathematical
or coding tasks

00:59:49.200 --> 00:59:52.280
compared to the instant
response mode, which

00:59:52.280 --> 00:59:57.280
is much more of the classic
instruction following plus RLHF

00:59:57.280 --> 00:59:58.020
models.

01:00:00.840 --> 01:00:04.680
And so you can-- one of the nice
things they do for us is look

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:07.760
at the contribution of all
of the different components

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:09.040
to performance.

01:00:09.040 --> 01:00:11.240
And in many ways, this
is not something that

01:00:11.240 --> 01:00:13.600
should surprise you too much.

01:00:13.600 --> 01:00:19.070
If you do this reasoning
RL and then general RL,

01:00:19.070 --> 01:00:21.790
you're able to slowly
get improvements

01:00:21.790 --> 01:00:25.070
in all of the different
tasks, things like Arena-Hard

01:00:25.070 --> 01:00:26.050
or CounterFactQA.

01:00:26.050 --> 01:00:30.050
These are general tasks for
which you want more normal RF,

01:00:30.050 --> 01:00:32.510
and we see significant
boosts to that performance

01:00:32.510 --> 01:00:34.350
across the board.

01:00:34.350 --> 01:00:37.270
There are some degradations
that we see in math and coding

01:00:37.270 --> 01:00:40.570
because we fuse together
non-thinking components,

01:00:40.570 --> 01:00:43.670
but the degradation
is not so bad.

01:00:43.670 --> 01:00:46.430
I think, actually, even
though these numbers

01:00:46.430 --> 01:00:50.010
in an absolute sense are
small in later releases,

01:00:50.010 --> 01:00:52.670
I think in some of
the Qwen 3.5, they've

01:00:52.670 --> 01:00:54.430
gone back on fusing
both thinking

01:00:54.430 --> 01:00:56.750
and non-thinking
into a single model.

01:00:56.750 --> 01:00:59.870
I think what-- they used to call
this hybrid models because they

01:00:59.870 --> 01:01:02.483
found this drop unacceptable.

01:01:02.483 --> 01:01:04.150
They wanted to squeeze
out all the juice

01:01:04.150 --> 01:01:05.690
possible on thinking modes.

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:07.950
And so they've now,
I think, separated

01:01:07.950 --> 01:01:10.870
these models from each other.

01:01:10.870 --> 01:01:19.500
The very last topic and paper
that I want to cover is Qwen 3--

01:01:19.500 --> 01:01:20.760
or sorry, I flipped the two.

01:01:20.760 --> 01:01:25.780
It's Coder-Next not Next-Coder,
which is one of the newer models

01:01:25.780 --> 01:01:26.680
to come out.

01:01:26.680 --> 01:01:29.425
And I think if you're
interested in general agent

01:01:29.425 --> 01:01:31.300
stuff, of course, there's
other agents things

01:01:31.300 --> 01:01:32.655
to think about harnesses.

01:01:32.655 --> 01:01:35.280
But if you're interested in how
do you actually build an agent.

01:01:35.280 --> 01:01:37.500
This is probably a
good report to go read.

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:43.780
Qwen3-Coder-Next great
agent post-training paper.

01:01:43.780 --> 01:01:46.020
But in some ways,
post-training for an agent

01:01:46.020 --> 01:01:48.640
is not very different from
everything that we've described.

01:01:48.640 --> 01:01:52.100
It's not like there's a new
agent training algorithm.

01:01:52.100 --> 01:01:54.980
Really, you should
internalize this lesson

01:01:54.980 --> 01:01:56.200
throughout the entire class.

01:01:56.200 --> 01:01:58.020
Data is the important thing.

01:01:58.020 --> 01:02:00.760
So if you're going to train an
agent or post-training an agent,

01:02:00.760 --> 01:02:01.820
what are you going to do?

01:02:01.820 --> 01:02:03.820
You're going to get
the data together.

01:02:03.820 --> 01:02:06.100
So there's two
parts to the data.

01:02:06.100 --> 01:02:08.400
To get a lot of
the capabilities,

01:02:08.400 --> 01:02:10.700
you can't just inject
them at the end.

01:02:10.700 --> 01:02:13.050
You have to probably start
a little bit earlier.

01:02:13.050 --> 01:02:15.290
And so there's an extensive
mid-training phase

01:02:15.290 --> 01:02:17.890
that happens to try
to get as much coding

01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:21.595
and agent like capabilities
into the model as possible.

01:02:21.595 --> 01:02:22.970
So they'll do
things like they'll

01:02:22.970 --> 01:02:25.890
take repositories and
concatenate the files

01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:28.970
in the repository to generate
these very long context data

01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:30.170
and put it into the model.

01:02:30.170 --> 01:02:31.810
We know that the
model is eventually

01:02:31.810 --> 01:02:34.410
going to see these very
long COT traces, where

01:02:34.410 --> 01:02:37.050
maybe the agent has opened
a bunch of files, very

01:02:37.050 --> 01:02:40.170
useful pre-training style data.

01:02:40.170 --> 01:02:42.570
They take pull
requests and then they

01:02:42.570 --> 01:02:45.070
try to construct
synthetic context for it,

01:02:45.070 --> 01:02:48.210
using RAG that is potentially
helpful for understanding

01:02:48.210 --> 01:02:49.310
the pull request.

01:02:49.310 --> 01:02:51.770
That all goes in
the mid training.

01:02:51.770 --> 01:02:55.970
They will take documents that
they detect using an automated

01:02:55.970 --> 01:02:58.130
method to have text and
code, and then they'll

01:02:58.130 --> 01:03:01.790
use an LLM to transform this
to a nice markdown format.

01:03:01.790 --> 01:03:03.810
Throw that all in as well.

01:03:03.810 --> 01:03:08.530
And then finally, they're going
to have a language model, talk

01:03:08.530 --> 01:03:11.720
about coding on web documents
that are coding related

01:03:11.720 --> 01:03:14.440
to generate coding-ish
synthetic data,

01:03:14.440 --> 01:03:16.960
and then they'll take
publicly-available coding

01:03:16.960 --> 01:03:19.180
agents, run them on
various environments,

01:03:19.180 --> 01:03:20.420
which I'll describe later.

01:03:20.420 --> 01:03:24.023
And the traces of those runs
all go into mid training.

01:03:24.023 --> 01:03:25.940
They'll do some fairly
standard stuff as well,

01:03:25.940 --> 01:03:27.565
like instruction
following, and they'll

01:03:27.565 --> 01:03:29.280
have an additional
task, which I think

01:03:29.280 --> 01:03:33.100
is not super relevant for us
for filling in the middle.

01:03:33.100 --> 01:03:35.765
If you're coding and you want
to fill in the middle of a span,

01:03:35.765 --> 01:03:37.140
it's useful to
have that ability.

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:40.160
So they throw some of that
data into mid training.

01:03:40.160 --> 01:03:45.320
But otherwise, this is a very
standard but in-depth data

01:03:45.320 --> 01:03:46.880
collection procedure
to try to get

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:50.360
some of these agentic
abilities into mid training.

01:03:50.360 --> 01:03:52.640
And then the Qwen
folks do something

01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:55.220
that I don't know
if I've seen before.

01:03:55.220 --> 01:03:57.640
It's actually quite interesting.

01:03:57.640 --> 01:04:01.332
They'll take the mid-trained
Qwen3 next model,

01:04:01.332 --> 01:04:03.040
and they are actually
just going to train

01:04:03.040 --> 01:04:04.720
a whole bunch of
different expert models

01:04:04.720 --> 01:04:08.400
for different kinds of
coding adjacent tasks.

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:12.878
And they will end up with
four different agent experts,

01:04:12.878 --> 01:04:14.670
and then they're just
going to distill them

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:17.030
all back into the same model.

01:04:17.030 --> 01:04:19.770
I can only speculate
as to why they do this.

01:04:19.770 --> 01:04:22.090
I don't think I've
seen it in other works.

01:04:22.090 --> 01:04:27.070
The closest one I know of
is DeepSeek V3 or was it V3?

01:04:27.070 --> 01:04:31.730
V3.5, sorry,
DeepSeek V3.5 or 3.2.

01:04:31.730 --> 01:04:32.370
Maybe 3.2.

01:04:32.370 --> 01:04:34.350
Sorry, 3.2, I think,
did this thing

01:04:34.350 --> 01:04:37.150
where they have different
experts whose only job it

01:04:37.150 --> 01:04:39.630
is to format and
process data, so they

01:04:39.630 --> 01:04:41.990
have data processing
experts which generate

01:04:41.990 --> 01:04:43.890
the data for the full training.

01:04:43.890 --> 01:04:46.190
But I don't think
I've seen this thing

01:04:46.190 --> 01:04:48.240
of generating
different model experts

01:04:48.240 --> 01:04:49.490
and then distilling them back.

01:04:49.490 --> 01:04:52.230
That's actually closer to some
academic work like branch train

01:04:52.230 --> 01:04:53.547
merge and so on.

01:04:53.547 --> 01:04:55.130
Those are definitely
things people do.

01:04:55.130 --> 01:04:58.390
I haven't seen it much in
frontier model training.

01:04:58.390 --> 01:05:01.653
That said, each of these
experts is actually interesting,

01:05:01.653 --> 01:05:03.070
and what they do
is they basically

01:05:03.070 --> 01:05:07.540
do full on RL or
and/or SFT training

01:05:07.540 --> 01:05:10.800
on a whole bunch of different
subtasks that they've defined.

01:05:10.800 --> 01:05:13.460
So they have a web
dev expert, where

01:05:13.460 --> 01:05:18.080
they SFT on valid web code based
on different kinds of checks.

01:05:18.080 --> 01:05:21.180
They'll do things
build UX experts

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:25.520
trained on many different tool
formats, and they'll have QA.

01:05:25.520 --> 01:05:31.860
So the QA agent is just trained
on more synthetic data for code.

01:05:31.860 --> 01:05:34.700
But then maybe the
most interesting for us

01:05:34.700 --> 01:05:40.100
and the most involved is their
software engineering agent.

01:05:40.100 --> 01:05:43.460
And what they do is they
construct agent environments

01:05:43.460 --> 01:05:44.780
at scale.

01:05:44.780 --> 01:05:47.940
So three benches is
the gold standard

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:49.160
of these kinds of things.

01:05:49.160 --> 01:05:52.060
And so what they want to do is
they want three bench but more.

01:05:52.060 --> 01:05:54.900
They go out and they have
various automated ways

01:05:54.900 --> 01:05:59.360
based on GitHub to generate
a ton of different issues.

01:05:59.360 --> 01:06:02.500
And given this, they
want to basically do

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:07.450
RL on these environments in
some ways very unsurprising.

01:06:07.450 --> 01:06:08.670
And what do they get?

01:06:08.670 --> 01:06:11.810
They can get RL
performance to go up.

01:06:11.810 --> 01:06:13.650
One thing I will
say here, and this

01:06:13.650 --> 01:06:17.010
is very important through the
broader context of what we're

01:06:17.010 --> 01:06:20.530
talking about, the reason why
we can put more and more compute

01:06:20.530 --> 01:06:23.210
into RL is because we
believe that our reward

01:06:23.210 --> 01:06:26.530
models are unhackable
or difficult to hack.

01:06:26.530 --> 01:06:29.530
If that assumption breaks
down, your RL method

01:06:29.530 --> 01:06:33.730
will find increasingly obscure
ways of cheating you out

01:06:33.730 --> 01:06:35.450
of your performance.

01:06:35.450 --> 01:06:40.990
And so they have this great
plot of basically, in Git,

01:06:40.990 --> 01:06:44.370
you can look at future
commits or different commits.

01:06:44.370 --> 01:06:46.990
And if you have an issue
and there's future commits,

01:06:46.990 --> 01:06:49.450
you can just look
up what the fix was.

01:06:49.450 --> 01:06:53.370
And this is a very easy
hack for models to learn.

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.050
And so they basically
have a whole reward

01:06:56.050 --> 01:06:59.890
whose entire purpose it is to
prevent the agent from messing

01:06:59.890 --> 01:07:01.250
with the Git history.

01:07:01.250 --> 01:07:03.050
And if you don't
do this, you end up

01:07:03.050 --> 01:07:05.710
with a plot that
looks like the right,

01:07:05.710 --> 01:07:08.210
where it's learning, learning,
learning, learning, learning.

01:07:08.210 --> 01:07:11.190
And suddenly, you get
this emergent jump, where

01:07:11.190 --> 01:07:12.990
the emergent jump
was actually, it

01:07:12.990 --> 01:07:17.270
learned how to manipulate the
Git calls to get the history.

01:07:17.270 --> 01:07:21.470
And in some cases, it can even
hack around certain constraints

01:07:21.470 --> 01:07:21.970
you give it.

01:07:21.970 --> 01:07:24.470
If you tell it, you
can't use Git log,

01:07:24.470 --> 01:07:27.430
it might add an origin like
a remote and then query

01:07:27.430 --> 01:07:30.478
the remote for what
happened in certain commits.

01:07:30.478 --> 01:07:32.270
So there's all sorts
of hacking you can do.

01:07:32.270 --> 01:07:36.750
RLVR is only as
robust as your reward,

01:07:36.750 --> 01:07:41.750
and your rewards can sometimes
be not very robust at all.

01:07:41.750 --> 01:07:44.910
As a side comment, one
of my students and I

01:07:44.910 --> 01:07:48.690
were working on a project
that was like RL on lean.

01:07:48.690 --> 01:07:51.950
Lean is this formal math,
verifiable language.

01:07:51.950 --> 01:07:54.050
And we naively
thought at the time,

01:07:54.050 --> 01:07:55.750
there's no way
this can go wrong.

01:07:55.750 --> 01:07:57.330
Lots of people have
worked on Lean.

01:07:57.330 --> 01:07:59.390
The Lean compiler
is bulletproof.

01:07:59.390 --> 01:08:02.490
Turns out the lean compiler
is not adversarially robust.

01:08:02.490 --> 01:08:03.980
There are strings
that you can put

01:08:03.980 --> 01:08:05.743
in it that will
allow you to verify

01:08:05.743 --> 01:08:08.160
proofs that are not meant to
be verified in certain modes.

01:08:08.160 --> 01:08:11.420
So I think the notion
of verifiable rewards

01:08:11.420 --> 01:08:13.660
is actually much
trickier than many of you

01:08:13.660 --> 01:08:15.580
might initially think.

01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:18.979
Regardless, you do this process
of constructing these GitHub

01:08:18.979 --> 01:08:20.300
repos at scale.

01:08:20.300 --> 01:08:22.899
You go through this
process of doing RL,

01:08:22.899 --> 01:08:27.880
you see your scores go up slow
but steady, and in the end,

01:08:27.880 --> 01:08:31.380
you will actually end up getting
a system that is surprisingly

01:08:31.380 --> 01:08:34.740
good, a model that
achieves something

01:08:34.740 --> 01:08:38.899
like 70.6% on SWE-bench,
even though you have

01:08:38.899 --> 01:08:42.300
what is basically a really
tiny model, a three billion

01:08:42.300 --> 01:08:45.460
active parameter model,
you're able to do surprisingly

01:08:45.460 --> 01:08:47.460
well on these tasks.

01:08:47.460 --> 01:08:49.300
In some ways,
though, I don't know

01:08:49.300 --> 01:08:51.120
if this is super surprising.

01:08:51.120 --> 01:08:52.580
RL, of course,
you're going to be

01:08:52.580 --> 01:08:55.100
able to do well on the
environments for which you've

01:08:55.100 --> 01:08:55.885
trained.

01:08:55.885 --> 01:08:57.260
You might even
get generalization

01:08:57.260 --> 01:08:59.973
to your validation
set as you do here.

01:08:59.973 --> 01:09:01.890
But you always want to
be a little bit careful

01:09:01.890 --> 01:09:03.689
about comparing performances.

01:09:03.689 --> 01:09:06.090
Task specific performance
doesn't necessarily

01:09:06.090 --> 01:09:11.090
mean it'll generalize
to broader domains.

01:09:11.090 --> 01:09:12.529
OK.

01:09:12.529 --> 01:09:16.609
So that's basically all I've
got for the RLVR lecture.

01:09:16.609 --> 01:09:19.970
Remember the core
takeaways of this lecture

01:09:19.970 --> 01:09:22.990
is it's really all
about the reward for RL.

01:09:22.990 --> 01:09:27.569
RLHF and RLVR arguably
they're very similar problems.

01:09:27.569 --> 01:09:31.130
But with the difference
being really just

01:09:31.130 --> 01:09:35.330
we want more unhackable
rewards so that we can actually

01:09:35.330 --> 01:09:39.330
put in much more compute and get
these systems to be much better.

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:42.029
GRPO, at least for the
research community,

01:09:42.029 --> 01:09:44.790
is something that really
enabled a lot of this.

01:09:44.790 --> 01:09:46.729
And you should all
know GRPO very well.

01:09:46.729 --> 01:09:48.810
You should know what
the functional form is

01:09:48.810 --> 01:09:50.130
and how the updates are.

01:09:50.130 --> 01:09:51.713
This is something
that you should know

01:09:51.713 --> 01:09:55.410
as well just
pre-training losses.

01:09:55.410 --> 01:09:58.270
And then finally, at this
point, I think lots of people

01:09:58.270 --> 01:10:00.760
know how to do RLVR.

01:10:00.760 --> 01:10:02.880
As you will find out,
unfortunately, RL

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:06.320
remains very finicky and noisy
and it's painful to work with.

01:10:06.320 --> 01:10:07.740
But it's not that hard.

01:10:07.740 --> 01:10:12.440
It's not like the old days of
doing PPO on various really

01:10:12.440 --> 01:10:13.660
tricky environments.

01:10:13.660 --> 01:10:18.820
It's actually a lot smoother
than you might think.

01:10:18.820 --> 01:10:21.160
I'll stop here for a moment
and take any final questions

01:10:21.160 --> 01:10:24.600
before I let you all go, in
case anyone has questions.

01:10:24.600 --> 01:10:26.520
A really quick
question-- when you

01:10:26.520 --> 01:10:30.100
mention about the models being
in thinking mode on and off,

01:10:30.100 --> 01:10:33.140
is the wait on the model
or [INAUDIBLE] the same?

01:10:33.140 --> 01:10:36.440
Or do they play prompting or do
they have different underlying

01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:38.900
models that they call back?

01:10:38.900 --> 01:10:39.400
Yeah.

01:10:39.400 --> 01:10:41.025
So the question was
about thinking mode

01:10:41.025 --> 01:10:42.520
and what's happening
on the back.

01:10:42.520 --> 01:10:45.240
So the interesting thing
about thinking mode really

01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:46.740
is that it's actually one model.

01:10:46.740 --> 01:10:48.320
And there's just a
little prompt tag

01:10:48.320 --> 01:10:51.160
that switches them between
long and short, like long COT

01:10:51.160 --> 01:10:53.360
and no coat almost mode.

01:10:53.360 --> 01:10:56.920
Whereas if you just had an
API flag or something, that's

01:10:56.920 --> 01:10:58.720
not very difficult.
The fact that

01:10:58.720 --> 01:11:00.470
they were putting both
of them in together

01:11:00.470 --> 01:11:02.030
is the interesting bit there.

01:11:02.030 --> 01:11:03.990
So you can actually
choose, [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:03.990 --> 01:11:07.190
but then you add extra
prompt yourself to do COT?

01:11:07.190 --> 01:11:10.270
Yeah, or the control mechanism
in this case is in the prompt

01:11:10.270 --> 01:11:12.630
rather than at the API or
serving layer or something

01:11:12.630 --> 01:11:13.270
like that.

01:11:13.270 --> 01:11:13.830
Yeah.

01:11:13.830 --> 01:11:15.030
Great.

01:11:15.030 --> 01:11:16.710
Oh, yeah.

01:11:16.710 --> 01:11:18.730
So you mentioned the
phase of the mid training.

01:11:18.730 --> 01:11:21.750
I was wondering how much
of the formatting that

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:23.910
goes into the mid
training process,

01:11:23.910 --> 01:11:27.270
informs what is able to be
learned during the RLVR post

01:11:27.270 --> 01:11:31.230
training and then as a secondary
to that question of if you don't

01:11:31.230 --> 01:11:33.810
have the correct
data in mid-training,

01:11:33.810 --> 01:11:37.310
then is it safe to assume that
the reinforcement learning can't

01:11:37.310 --> 01:11:39.950
sample a correct solution and
therefore you can't learn it?

01:11:39.950 --> 01:11:40.450
Yeah.

01:11:40.450 --> 01:11:43.990
So the question was about the
role of mid-training in RL.

01:11:43.990 --> 01:11:48.450
I think there are a lot of
empirical subtleties here.

01:11:48.450 --> 01:11:50.530
I don't have a
one size fits all.

01:11:50.530 --> 01:11:52.290
It will definitely
not work an answer,

01:11:52.290 --> 01:11:54.070
but to give a little
bit of the nuance,

01:11:54.070 --> 01:11:56.820
I think pre-training
and SFT are doing

01:11:56.820 --> 01:11:58.000
a lot of the heavy lifting.

01:11:58.000 --> 01:11:59.740
As long as you have coverage,
if pre-training just

01:11:59.740 --> 01:12:01.865
doesn't have any code data,
then you're in trouble.

01:12:01.865 --> 01:12:02.825
You need training.

01:12:02.825 --> 01:12:05.200
But if your pre-training was
very diverse and it covered,

01:12:05.200 --> 01:12:07.620
let's go back to the
mid-training slide.

01:12:07.620 --> 01:12:10.700
If it covered a lot of
stuff like text code data

01:12:10.700 --> 01:12:13.780
and it covered a
bunch of GitHub data,

01:12:13.780 --> 01:12:17.820
then this is very nice to
have, but maybe not critical

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:19.820
because you're
going to SFT anyway,

01:12:19.820 --> 01:12:22.620
and the SFT will allow you
to get close enough to start

01:12:22.620 --> 01:12:24.120
getting some rewards for RL.

01:12:24.120 --> 01:12:26.880
If you didn't have SFT, then
you're in DeepSeek trouble.

01:12:26.880 --> 01:12:28.380
But the fact that
we're always going

01:12:28.380 --> 01:12:31.620
to be doing some amount of
SFT pre RL really helps you,

01:12:31.620 --> 01:12:33.280
and pre-training
has enough coverage,

01:12:33.280 --> 01:12:34.940
hopefully that
you're able to get

01:12:34.940 --> 01:12:36.560
some reasonable generalization.

01:12:36.560 --> 01:12:38.420
So mid-training is
very nice to have.

01:12:38.420 --> 01:12:42.540
Very important in order to get
slightly better generalization

01:12:42.540 --> 01:12:44.840
but not necessarily
make or break, I think.

01:12:48.540 --> 01:12:49.500
Yeah.

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:53.520
[INAUDIBLE] there is a step
after train your transfer model.

01:12:53.520 --> 01:12:57.370
We're going to be still jumping
into a single model at the end.

01:12:57.370 --> 01:13:00.950
So does that-- how does this
kind of distillation work?

01:13:00.950 --> 01:13:06.530
Do you have to design
a mixed procedure?

01:13:06.530 --> 01:13:07.250
Yeah.

01:13:07.250 --> 01:13:08.050
So that's right.

01:13:08.050 --> 01:13:09.650
So distillation
procedure is going

01:13:09.650 --> 01:13:12.170
to require you to write
down a sequence of prompts

01:13:12.170 --> 01:13:15.110
on which the experts will be
distilled into the final model.

01:13:15.110 --> 01:13:16.370
Yeah.

01:13:16.370 --> 01:13:18.170
I can definitely
see the advantages

01:13:18.170 --> 01:13:19.990
of this approach,
which is that you

01:13:19.990 --> 01:13:21.990
can have a separate team
working on each expert.

01:13:21.990 --> 01:13:25.370
It's much easier to
have teams work on this.

01:13:25.370 --> 01:13:27.870
And then the aggregation could
potentially be simple as well

01:13:27.870 --> 01:13:29.690
if you have enough compute.

01:13:29.690 --> 01:13:33.012
It's just that this-- if
you have all the objectives,

01:13:33.012 --> 01:13:35.470
you might as well just throw
it into the big training loop.

01:13:35.470 --> 01:13:37.430
Usually, I think that's how
you would prefer to do things.

01:13:37.430 --> 01:13:39.530
That way, you don't have to
deal with this distillation

01:13:39.530 --> 01:13:40.110
complication.

01:13:40.110 --> 01:13:42.610
You could just have one big
training objective that you then

01:13:42.610 --> 01:13:46.330
do one training loop.

01:13:46.330 --> 01:13:47.230
Yeah.

01:13:47.230 --> 01:13:49.376
Is the long reasoning
training also part

01:13:49.376 --> 01:13:52.195
of the training,
that mid-training?

01:13:52.195 --> 01:13:54.320
So the question was, is
the long reasoning training

01:13:54.320 --> 01:13:56.120
part of mid-training.

01:13:56.120 --> 01:14:05.440
So I think so there's long COT
SFT in both R1 and Kimi K5.1.

01:14:05.440 --> 01:14:09.100
Long COT is not traditionally
part of mid-training, per se,

01:14:09.100 --> 01:14:10.560
but it is often--

01:14:10.560 --> 01:14:13.605
long COT like data is used
in long context extension.

01:14:13.605 --> 01:14:14.980
So it's a little
bit more subtle.

01:14:14.980 --> 01:14:17.640
I didn't talk about long
context extension at all

01:14:17.640 --> 01:14:19.680
and I regret that.

01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:22.080
But usually, that's an
additional phase right

01:14:22.080 --> 01:14:25.220
before RLHF or other
things, in this case,

01:14:25.220 --> 01:14:28.600
also long COT where you
extend the context using

01:14:28.600 --> 01:14:30.140
any long context data you have.

01:14:30.140 --> 01:14:33.358
Usually, it's a combination of
books, code, synthetic data,

01:14:33.358 --> 01:14:35.400
because those are the
things that are long enough

01:14:35.400 --> 01:14:38.520
to do extension on.

01:14:38.520 --> 01:14:40.280
Yeah.

01:14:40.280 --> 01:14:43.640
So like I guess related to this
diagram that's currently up,

01:14:43.640 --> 01:14:45.500
when you're doing this
[INAUDIBLE] process,

01:14:45.500 --> 01:14:48.880
let's say how much math,
chemistry, many other domains.

01:14:48.880 --> 01:14:52.630
Is it common to do it
in parallel, with one,

01:14:52.630 --> 01:14:58.390
I guess, like reinforcement
learning training run?

01:14:58.390 --> 01:15:00.650
Or you do it sequentially?

01:15:00.650 --> 01:15:02.390
And if you do it
sequentially, how

01:15:02.390 --> 01:15:05.828
do you avoid forgetting
earlier parts of the learning?

01:15:05.828 --> 01:15:07.870
Yeah, I think the closest
answer to this question

01:15:07.870 --> 01:15:10.390
do you do things
sequentially or in parallel

01:15:10.390 --> 01:15:14.830
is these diagrams, where
the two splits that you

01:15:14.830 --> 01:15:16.910
do is you split
amongst like reasoning

01:15:16.910 --> 01:15:18.912
problems and
non-reasoning problems,

01:15:18.912 --> 01:15:20.870
and the reasoning problems
all go in one bucket

01:15:20.870 --> 01:15:23.310
and they get
executed at stage II.

01:15:23.310 --> 01:15:25.830
And then the non
reasoning problems

01:15:25.830 --> 01:15:28.125
happen at the final RLHF phase.

01:15:28.125 --> 01:15:29.750
And this includes
stuff like chattiness

01:15:29.750 --> 01:15:30.708
and so on and so forth.

01:15:30.708 --> 01:15:32.850
And those all go into
the one later bit,

01:15:32.850 --> 01:15:34.130
which is the stage IV.

01:15:38.850 --> 01:15:39.350
OK.

01:15:39.350 --> 01:15:40.350
Great.

01:15:40.350 --> 01:15:41.550
Thanks a lot.

01:15:41.550 --> 01:15:42.330
See you next week.

01:15:42.330 --> 01:15:45.080
[APPLAUSE]

